WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.390
- [Judith] My name's Judith Resnick.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.343
I'm the Arthur Liman Professor
of Law at Yale Law School.

00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:15.270
We, the Liman Center,
along with Dwayne Betts,

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.000
the Liman Center being Jennifer Taylor,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.614
shout out here who's here.

00:00:19.614 --> 00:00:21.540
There's a report about us

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.960
and if anybody wants to know
more than I wish we could,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.000
we had to tell you about
solitary confinement.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.490
There's a report called
"Time in Cell" just out

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.310
about more than 50,000 people
in solitary confinement

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.840
in the United States.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.443
So we, Freedom Reads, Peter, Diane,

00:00:39.102 --> 00:00:41.880
Whitney Humanities plus plus,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.610
are all very glad that you're all here.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.270
I was actually, at the
law school right now

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.239
there's another conference
called Access and Accountability

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:54.360
run by what is called the Mafia,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.260
the Media Freedom of Information Clinic.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.480
And I wish that all 60 of those people

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.040
could be also sitting here.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.670
And I did pitch "please
think about and talk about

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.040
and write about, and thank
goodness you do some,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.001
the state of incarceration."

00:01:10.001 --> 00:01:12.330
I'm gonna start by introducing

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.410
some of the themes of the
panel and our discussion

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.170
with a little bit of pictorial and text

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.630
while also, and then thereafter,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.390
each of our panelists is going to speak

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.060
in the order in which
they're seated, actually.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.060
Elizabeth Hinton, and I'm not gonna,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.550
I think everybody's bios are available,

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.860
so I'm not gonna talk about who they are

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:35.820
'cause you're gonna listen to them

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.320
rather than me talk about them.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.373
And Ekow Yankah and Zachary?

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:43.853
- [Zachary] Lazar.

00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:44.910
- Lazar, sorry.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.800
There's a law school
person who has that name.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.570
Anyway, and Tracy Meares.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.580
I was gonna add alter names.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.960
But I wanna start by talking about

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.610
the degree to which incarceration
has been very visible,

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:04.610
is very visible and has been
for centuries as a practice,

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.610
that it is visible to those
in and outside of prisons

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.390
for any of the many public minds or eyes

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.480
who want to look or who care to look.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.540
And not only that, but
the fact of incarceration

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.540
has been for a long term
advertised as well as debated.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.808
And the practices of incarceration

00:02:25.808 --> 00:02:29.277
and the practices of punishment are here.

00:02:29.277 --> 00:02:32.730
And I wanna invite, 'cause
it's a wonderful audience,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.730
to think about the range
of text and materials

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:36.900
that are available.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.817
So assuming, there, I'm
in Amsterdam in the 1780s,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.660
the population is declining.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.300
And there is a folio which,
thank you the Beinecke.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.770
And I also need to advertise that

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.090
there's an exhibit at the Beinecke,

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.450
an exhibit at the law
school on some images

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.640
that you can go see

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.860
And I have some handouts for that.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.860
So this image is one of
about a hundred in a folio

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.090
put together to tell
you "Move to Amsterdam.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.350
It's a great town."

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.710
20% of the images are about
how they detain people,

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.110
either in orphanages, poor people,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.210
and this is the men's house of correction,

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.670
which is the largest of the buildings

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.290
of that era being built.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.550
And this is the ad.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.700
This is not anything covert whatsoever.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.957
And when you go inside, and
thank you Bernard Harcour

00:03:26.957 --> 00:03:30.311
'cause this one's in color,
the color image is fabulous.

00:03:30.311 --> 00:03:33.070
You're watching somebody being flogged.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.507
And this is the ad.

00:03:34.507 --> 00:03:37.050
"We keep control, we're
very tidy, we're neat,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.460
we make you do menial work

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.100
and we whip you if you don't behave.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.173
Move to Amsterdam."

00:03:43.173 --> 00:03:46.256
(audience chuckling)

00:03:47.132 --> 00:03:50.325
Now, as of course the invention
of the penitentiary comes,

00:03:50.325 --> 00:03:52.070
of course starts to be some concerns.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.340
So not all the visuals look like this.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.310
And if you go to the Yale
Center for British Art,

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:59.190
you'll find this, which is a print after

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.287
a painting by Thomas Ryder of the captive.

00:04:02.287 --> 00:04:05.850
The Brits are depicting
someone in a French prison,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.140
the Channel fight.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:12.140
And it's pointing to this kind
of saintly, chained prisoner

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.243
to start the call for reform,

00:04:15.243 --> 00:04:20.243
and across the continent
and then across the oceans.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.410
And this is Europe and the United States.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.961
Not all the world by any
stretch of the imagination,

00:04:24.961 --> 00:04:27.701
although there's a colonial piece.

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:31.501
The efforts at religious
societies to reform,

00:04:31.501 --> 00:04:34.290
improve the lot of prisoners,

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:37.470
ramps up with the founding
of social sciences,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.240
of sociology and anthropology

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.720
and this thing called criminology.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.300
And the next stop is now a
plaque that was put up in 1970,

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.580
but it commemorates in 1870,

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.737
the first ever national
Congressional Congress

00:04:50.737 --> 00:04:53.580
where the people who were the prison men

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.350
met with Rutherford B. Hayes,

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.462
welcoming him as the governor,
before he became president,

00:04:57.462 --> 00:04:58.680
of Ohio,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.420
to say welcome to these
organizations that are committed to

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.600
figuring out how to
run and reform prisons.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.360
And unlike the early part of the 1800s

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.130
when we really wanted menial work,

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.320
now we're into reformation
and useful work,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.450
some of it of course hugely profit-making

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.050
for the people who are doing it.

00:05:18.097 --> 00:05:19.533
They talk, they debate.

00:05:19.533 --> 00:05:22.440
You can read, I've read hundreds
and hundreds and hundreds

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.300
of pages of their materials.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.920
How you punish, which way you punish?

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.540
Do you crook, do you da-da-da-da?

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:32.340
And here's an example from
a British prison of 1902

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.220
and you're looking at a
disgusting practice called

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.290
what they called a tread wheel.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:40.710
Men being made to go circle seven steps

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.870
over and over and over again.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:47.084
While this is all displayed,
available, seeable

00:05:47.084 --> 00:05:48.960
maybe for a deterrent,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.080
Jeremy Bentham wanted you all
to be scared and not do this.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.060
So he wanted the panopticon.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:58.920
So this is the 1920s.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.389
This is a painting of Marjorie Fry.

00:06:02.389 --> 00:06:05.550
Part of it is that in a
world of gender stories,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:09.570
this gender story needs to
include that there are reformers,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.035
some of whom are women,
some of whom are feminists,

00:06:12.035 --> 00:06:17.035
and Fry and her colleagues of
the Howard League in London

00:06:17.316 --> 00:06:21.000
are the first speakers I've
found in a variety of documents

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.227
that say the words "rights" and "torture."

00:06:23.227 --> 00:06:25.320
"Prison is torture and
people deserve rights

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.480
and there should be an
international convention."

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.370
And the guys who are by then running

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:32.490
the International Penal
Penitentiary Commission

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.890
poo-poo the ladies, they
call them "the ladies,"

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:35.940
and say "get rid of them."

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.970
But these are incredibly
entrepreneurial smart women

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.760
who go to Geneva and lobby
the League of Nations

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.800
with these amazing anti-death penalty,

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.918
pro "convention for the rights
of prisoners," their words.

00:06:47.918 --> 00:06:51.570
And therefore the guys at this
international organization

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.544
which is full of
countries that are empires

00:06:54.544 --> 00:06:57.090
say "we better draft rules."

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:01.500
And the women convince
the men who draft rules.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:05.100
And for the first time in 1935,
the League of Nations adopts

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.520
the standard minimum rules for
the treatment of prisoners,

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.240
which you see are from a
'humanitarian' point of view

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.800
and a 'social' point of view.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.410
And what their rules are
just give you heartburn

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.420
in terms of how awful prisons are.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.740
So there should be enough food,

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.030
i.e. there wasn't enough food.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:23.280
There should be enough water, et cetera.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.860
Corporal punishment is
generally not acceptable

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.990
except in exceptional cases

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.180
if a medical person authorizes it.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.040
That is because the Brits love whipping.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.800
It isn't just Amsterdam and
it isn't just elsewhere.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.300
The Brits and the British colonies

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.400
have flogging birching and whipping a lot.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.850
And in exceptional cases, a
dark cell, if regulated by law,

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:45.390
i.e. a dungeon is also
permissible as well.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.130
The Caesar rules are disseminated.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:48.900
This is the frontispiece
from the Home Office

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.000
with the Queen's seal,

00:07:50.864 --> 00:07:52.890
which tells you that we're
sending them around to everyone.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:56.176
And then World War II comes.

00:07:56.176 --> 00:07:59.220
This international
organization actually meets

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.140
in Berlin in the 1930s

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:05.910
because if prisoners have no
rights, because the UN did not,

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.640
the League of Nations had
no language of rights,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.220
then fascist, whatever your government is,

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.080
we can make things together

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.040
to manage the folks we
are keeping in detention,

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:19.380
but when you get to the
United Nations in 1955,

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.090
although these are not enforceable,

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.477
the new words come in:

00:08:22.477 --> 00:08:24.997
"in a world of rights,"
"non-discrimination," and

00:08:24.997 --> 00:08:28.350
"the person has a right to
self-determination and liberty."

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.126
But then there's the sad caveat:

00:08:30.126 --> 00:08:34.920
"unless we need to do something
to control and manage you."

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:37.200
And if you go through
some of their rights,

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.150
we are now, "discipline is needed."

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.710
We're banning corporal punishment.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:42.450
There's no longer a dark cell.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:44.430
But here's what we tolerate.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:48.471
We tolerate reduced diet as an
euphemism for bread and water

00:08:48.471 --> 00:08:51.480
and we tolerate solitary confinement

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:54.420
if it's certified that you can stand it.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.430
Now, of course in the United States

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.512
and elsewhere in the world

00:08:58.512 --> 00:08:59.490
in terms of colonial imperialism,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:03.480
of course the centrality
of race in this story.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.540
So there are many pioneers in
the United States in the 1960s

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:07.920
who are individual prisoners

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:11.640
who believe that law will help them.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.380
And they look at the US Constitution

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:14.940
and they say, "law can help me."

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.283
And in doing this, this
is Caleb Washington

00:09:17.283 --> 00:09:19.645
and the case is called Washington V Lee.

00:09:19.645 --> 00:09:22.980
Not George Washington versus Robert E.,

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.020
although the people who've
styled the complaint

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.090
knew exactly what they were doing.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:29.700
Alabama had a statute that said

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:32.700
segregation by race meant that

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.310
Black and white people
couldn't be chained together

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.410
or sleep together in prison.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:42.410
A lawsuit challenging this in
1966 wins in the lower courts

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:45.600
and in the US Supreme Court they bring

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.580
what they call "a statement of questions."

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.890
And the question they raise,

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.260
this is 1967 in the United States

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.330
and these people have the, the
Yiddish word is "chutzpah,"

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.416
to say, "Does a prisoner
serving a penitentiary sentence

00:09:57.416 --> 00:10:00.456
have a constitutional right
to question the validity of

00:10:00.456 --> 00:10:03.690
state statutes requiring
separation by race?"

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.420
Which is to tell you
the status of the person

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:08.760
was claimed to be not a person

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:11.430
who had any relationship
to the US Constitution

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:12.990
once it was you were convicted.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.180
And by the way, this is the first term

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.490
that Thurgood Marshall is
sitting in the US Supreme Court,

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.447
which is the first time

00:10:19.447 --> 00:10:21.360
it's not an all white institution itself.

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.250
So just to be quickly summarizing,

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:26.910
first of all, it's a
very visible activity.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.430
There are debates about
permissible and impermissible

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:32.656
and the freaks who, the people
who are the serious speakers

00:10:32.656 --> 00:10:36.296
of the claim for a
constraint on sovereign power

00:10:36.296 --> 00:10:38.160
are the folks who are incarcerated

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.080
who say "you can't treat me like this."

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:43.627
And the answer from
justice is, the court says,

00:10:43.627 --> 00:10:45.210
"Yes, you can't segregate by race

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.400
except, comes a concurrence,

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.580
if their racial tensions,
you can take them in account

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.890
to maintain security
discipline and good order

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.090
in prisons and jails."

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:55.237
That's the mantra.

00:10:55.237 --> 00:10:58.020
"Maintain security,
discipline, and good order."

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:00.530
So here come to Arkansas in 1967,

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:02.550
and this is a picture in the newspaper,

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.200
thank goodness for newspapers.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.570
And the newspaper says,
"This is the whip."

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:10.320
Arkansas says, "To maintain
good order, we whip prisoners."

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:13.674
And Winston Talley is a guy who says

00:11:13.674 --> 00:11:15.783
in this handwritten petition,

00:11:16.632 --> 00:11:18.450
"Please people writing,
look at the literature

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:20.910
produced by the people
who are in detention,"

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:22.290
handwriting in a variety of ways

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.367
not only to courts,
but to courts included,

00:11:24.367 --> 00:11:26.820
"whip, W H I P P, no evidence."

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:30.030
This is a man who's part of,
I've learned some about him,

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.620
that he's part of a social movement,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:33.768
but he understands himself.

00:11:33.768 --> 00:11:35.340
He says, "Article eight,
you can't whip me."

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:37.320
And he gets a hearing in federal court.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.360
This is the group of
people who are testifying

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.880
as Arkansas has about 60% white prisoners

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.820
and is whipping Black and
white people together.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:45.653
Now of course,

00:11:46.568 --> 00:11:47.935
while these people may or
may not be part of movements,

00:11:47.935 --> 00:11:50.670
there is of course a huge social movement

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.740
of which the Attica
picture is one example.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:55.215
The Nation of Islam is
organizing in prisons as well.

00:11:55.215 --> 00:11:59.293
And there's an effort at what
is profound and serious claims

00:11:59.293 --> 00:12:04.271
of the capacity of the
state to require the state

00:12:04.271 --> 00:12:06.870
to not punish people in particular ways.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.330
And you get for a little
while federal judges saying,

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.727
here's in the fifth Circuit
with Louisiana, Angola,

00:12:12.727 --> 00:12:14.820
"We are here, the Constitution's here,

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:16.980
you're in relationship
to the Constitution,

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:18.720
you're us as well."

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.020
But as is sadly familiar to many of you,

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.900
by the 1980s, we're not here very much.

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:29.220
And the new floor of what is
tolerable constitutionally

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:33.060
is "minimal civilized measure
of life's necessities."

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:35.580
A pathetic floor for this whole thing.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.940
And here, just to bring you
to date in '08 in California,

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:40.770
you have hyper density.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:43.708
Hyper density, which everybody,

00:12:43.708 --> 00:12:46.646
actually the word used
to be called "crowding."

00:12:46.646 --> 00:12:51.630
"Overcrowding" is a weird
word that gets added.

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:53.550
But if you go to the 1970s and '80s,

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.870
they call it "crowding."

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:56.850
So, overcrowding is here

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.388
and anybody who wants
to look at its harms,

00:12:59.388 --> 00:13:02.400
the Department of Justice,
this is the document that's out

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:04.830
that is, a trigger warning,
it's horrible to read

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.570
about Alabama state prisons.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.880
And of course it's appropriate
to invoke them because

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.700
the prisoners have just gone
on strike there in protests.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.920
You can read pages of this.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:17.104
So, it's not secret, there's
contestation about it,

00:13:17.104 --> 00:13:19.952
and the question about what is
permissible in a social order

00:13:19.952 --> 00:13:24.030
brings me to the last image,
which is by Faith Ringold,

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:26.526
called "The United States of Attica"

00:13:26.526 --> 00:13:29.820
marking the violence that is incomplete.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:32.370
The last sentence at the
bottom, which is hard to read,

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.860
says, "This map of American
violence is incomplete.

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:37.410
Please write in whatever
you find lacking."

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:40.116
Seems to me she is the
words to be the introduction

00:13:40.116 --> 00:13:43.050
to the people who are coming to speak.

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:46.378
And I'm incredibly grateful
that they've come and are here

00:13:46.378 --> 00:13:49.860
and are gonna talk to us some
about the problems of this,

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:51.750
which they can do either
standing up or sitting

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:53.754
depending on their backs and their spines.

00:13:53.754 --> 00:13:56.670
Go for it, Elizabeth, you're next.

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:58.080
Whatever you want.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:13:59.330
- I'm gonna sit, I think.

00:14:00.328 --> 00:14:01.455
- [Judith] Okay.

00:14:01.455 --> 00:14:02.876
- Yes, thank you for that.

00:14:02.876 --> 00:14:04.440
And thank you to the
Whitney Humanities Center,

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.750
Freedom Reads, the Liman Center,

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.990
and of course organizers
Judith Resnik, Dwayne Betts,

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:11.820
Jenny Carroll and Peter Brooks

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:14.070
for inviting me to join this conversation

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:15.960
and all of you for coming.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:18.960
So, picking up on this slide,

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:21.270
this is actually like the perfect segue.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:23.490
I wanna think about
the image of the prison

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:27.480
in the public mind in
the era of rebellion,

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:32.476
what could be seen as the opening
act of mass incarceration,

00:14:32.476 --> 00:14:36.630
because immediately after
the long hot summers of

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:39.150
the late 1960s and early 1970s,

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.730
people of color and Black
people in particular start

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:45.690
getting disproportionately
and systematically locked up

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.510
and mass incarceration
as we know it begins.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:51.810
So what I call the crucible
period of rebellion

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:54.669
lasted from 1964 to 1972,

00:14:54.669 --> 00:14:58.620
and every major city burned
during these eight years,

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:00.840
as well as midsize and smaller cities

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:03.207
and rural towns across the United States,

00:15:03.207 --> 00:15:05.730
East and West, from the North and South,

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:07.260
the Rust Belt to the Sun Belt,

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:10.320
leading to nearly 3,000 incidents in total

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:14.032
across more than 1,000
segregated communities of color.

00:15:14.032 --> 00:15:17.130
Now, nearly all of these
rebellions were sparked

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:22.130
by a police encounter, a
moment of tangibly felt racism,

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.800
yet the tens of thousands
of Black and Brown people

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:27.630
who threw rocks and bottles at police,

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:31.408
plundered local stores,
detonated Molotov cocktails

00:15:31.408 --> 00:15:35.040
were rebelling not just against
police brutality, right?

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:37.980
They were rebelling
against a broader system

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:40.500
that had entrenched unequal conditions

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.610
and anti-Black violence over generations.

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:47.910
So the so-called 'riots'
constituted a set of demands

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:50.670
for jobs, first and foremost,

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:53.040
expand in educational opportunities,

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:54.627
better housing conditions,

00:15:54.627 --> 00:15:57.750
and an end to state-sanctioned violence.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.880
These of course were the same demands as

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:02.430
the civil rights movement more broadly,

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:06.540
but policy makers treated the
rebellions as criminal acts,

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:08.660
as crime, not as calls for

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:12.951
full economic and political
inclusion in American society.

00:16:12.951 --> 00:16:15.930
So under the banner of the War on Crime,

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:17.700
which President Lyndon Johnson started

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:19.302
in the spring of 1965,

00:16:19.302 --> 00:16:22.284
the federal government supported
the expansion of police

00:16:22.284 --> 00:16:24.450
and targeted Black, Puerto Rican,

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:26.280
and Mexican-American communities,

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:29.700
accompanied by the militarization
of those police forces

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:32.430
with an arsenal of surplus
weapons and technologies

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:33.330
from Vietnam

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:36.480
and interventions in South
America and the Caribbean.

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:39.727
So the national solution,
the counterinsurgency,

00:16:39.727 --> 00:16:43.440
was more police, leading
to more incarceration.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.530
And the effect was to
place low-income families

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:50.867
and their communities under
the omnipresent gaze of police

00:16:50.867 --> 00:16:53.520
and to normalize confinement.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.790
Now, we often think of
police brutality as being

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:59.608
the proximate cause of
the collective violence,

00:16:59.608 --> 00:17:01.728
as was the response most recently

00:17:01.728 --> 00:17:05.880
to the brutal murders of George
Floyd and Breonna Taylor,

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:08.220
which sparked the nonviolent
and violent protests

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:10.830
that we witnessed during
the summer of 2020.

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:12.825
But the rebellions very often began

00:17:12.825 --> 00:17:16.824
in response to the
specter of incarceration.

00:17:16.824 --> 00:17:20.280
People fought back when police exercised

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:23.201
their most basic legal
authority: making arrests.

00:17:23.201 --> 00:17:24.990
According to data calculated by

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:27.000
the political scientist
Christian Davenport

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.060
at the University of Michigan's
Radical Information Project,

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:33.125
about 24% of the rebellions
during the crucible period

00:17:33.125 --> 00:17:36.434
began in response to an
arrest or an attempted arrest

00:17:36.434 --> 00:17:41.280
that was seen in the minds
of participants as arbitrary.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:45.630
The Watts Rebellion of
1965 and Newark in 1967,

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:47.487
which, along with Detroit of course, are

00:17:47.487 --> 00:17:51.367
the 'iconic riots' of the era
in the public imagination,

00:17:51.367 --> 00:17:55.830
each began in response to the
arrest of a Black motorist.

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:57.270
People did not want to see

00:17:57.270 --> 00:18:00.120
their friends, family, and
fellow community members

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:03.780
handcuffed, detained, captured,
removed from the community,

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:07.830
thrown behind bars, and possibly
sent to far away prison.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:09.630
In Baltimore, Black residents

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:12.091
challenged officers' arresting
authority with determination,

00:18:12.091 --> 00:18:15.240
disrupting arrests on
three separate occasions

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:18.120
in late June 1968, about two months after

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:20.820
the week-long rebellion that followed

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:23.484
the assassination of Martin Luther King.

00:18:23.484 --> 00:18:27.000
On June 19th, after an officer
tried to arrest a young man

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:28.830
in the heart of the
Black Upton neighborhood

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.450
in front of a growing crowd,

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:33.595
a woman scratched him down his
left arm and took his watch.

00:18:33.595 --> 00:18:36.480
The officer sprayed the
woman and others with mace

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:39.240
and the people who
gathered at the scene left.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:41.610
Less than a week later, a 15-year-old boy

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:44.488
grabbed an officer's arm during
a disorderly conduct arrest,

00:18:44.488 --> 00:18:47.730
giving his suspect an opportunity to flee.

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:50.668
The officer hit the boy
and took him into custody.

00:18:50.668 --> 00:18:54.150
The next evening, two dozen
teenagers began shouting

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:57.030
at officers Paul
Kirosavich and Norman Stamp

00:18:57.030 --> 00:19:00.906
as they attempted to arrest
three people on burglary charges

00:19:00.906 --> 00:19:04.710
at around 11:30 in the
Reservoir Hill neighborhood.

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:07.410
A 13-year-old girl jumped on Stamp's back,

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:10.560
forcing him to let go of
two of the of the suspects,

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:12.030
and the men escaped.

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:14.070
So Officer Stamp sees the girl,

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:17.220
who kicked him, clawed him, and bit him.

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:19.830
Eventually, Stamp managed
to put the girl in handcuffs

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:21.060
for resisting arrest,

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:23.880
but suddenly her 14-year-old brother

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.250
attacked the officer from behind.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:28.800
And after a struggle, the
brother was also detained.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:30.900
Another 14-year-old boy helped free

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:33.960
the third burglary suspect
by assaulting Kirosavich.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.720
The suspects, all three of
them young men, escaped,

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:39.426
and the kids who came to
their defense went to jail.

00:19:39.426 --> 00:19:42.125
Viewing the arrests as an injustice

00:19:42.125 --> 00:19:45.720
and the potential to be
incarcerated as an injustice,

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.430
these young people were willing to

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:49.890
take justice into their own hands

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:53.220
and sacrifice their own
freedom in the process.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:55.470
Although the informal
arrest prevention campaign

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.450
in Baltimore did not directly inspire

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:59.400
a broad rebellion in other cities,

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.340
a single arrest could lead to
sustained collective violence

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:04.320
committed by hundreds of people.

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:07.050
For example, in Jersey
City in August 1970,

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:09.746
about 500 Black and Puerto
Rican residents declared

00:20:09.746 --> 00:20:13.200
what police called
"open warfare on whites"

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.490
in the downtown area

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:16.500
after two patrolmen stopped a vehicle

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:18.840
for a minor traffic violation.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:22.650
Weeks later in nearby Hoboken,
a six-hour-long drug raid

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:24.930
during which 25 people were arrested,

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:26.433
caused a two-night rebellion.

00:20:27.979 --> 00:20:30.659
The prominent 21-year-old
activist Luis Lopez

00:20:30.659 --> 00:20:32.460
came to protest the mass arrest

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:35.947
and was subsequently arrested himself for

00:20:35.947 --> 00:20:37.710
"interfering with police duties"

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.780
and using "obscene language."

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:43.005
By 7:30 PM, 300 to 400
"enraged" Puerto Ricans,

00:20:43.005 --> 00:20:46.050
as the Hudson Post
Dispatch described them,

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:49.080
surrounded city hall,
demanding Lopez's release.

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:51.120
About 30 people sat at the intersection

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:53.730
of Washington and 1st to block traffic.

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:55.650
The crowd threw rocks
and bottles at police

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:58.050
and smashed windows in the area.

00:20:58.050 --> 00:20:59.628
Lopez continued to protest

00:20:59.628 --> 00:21:02.760
the arrest of Puerto Rican
residents in Hoboken,

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:05.190
leading to another rebellion a year later.

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:07.170
It started with an angry confrontation

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:10.373
between Luis and Jamie
Santana, aged 26 and 24,

00:21:10.373 --> 00:21:13.020
and a store owner who the brothers claimed

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:16.680
repossessed their television
set under false pretenses.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:18.690
After the Santana brothers were arrested

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.490
for assault with a dangerous weapon,

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:23.460
Lopez led a small group of
activists to police headquarters

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:25.560
to demand the release of the prisoners

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:28.320
and to request a suspension
of the two officers

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:31.710
who allegedly used brutal
force during the arrest.

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:33.720
Soon after Lopez and his group left,

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:35.970
police reportedly received
calls that people were

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:38.868
harassing motorists and passers
by outside of City Hall,

00:21:38.868 --> 00:21:40.890
and then when local officers arrived,

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:42.990
a group of about 300 people

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:45.570
began throwing cans,
bottles, and bricks at them,

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:47.691
and the rebellion spread from there.

00:21:47.691 --> 00:21:50.970
Seen as an unfair, unjust,
and racist institution,

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:53.635
the siblings in Baltimore,
Luis Lopez and his defenders

00:21:53.635 --> 00:21:55.770
and their counterparts in other cities

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:59.130
took the act of policing and
the resulting incarceration

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:00.600
as a form of state violence

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:04.525
that could only be contested
with further violence.

00:22:04.525 --> 00:22:07.500
The beginning of mass incarceration

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:10.050
helped bring the crucible
period of rebellion,

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:13.890
the period of frequent
community-based instinctive violence,

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:15.270
to an end.

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:17.880
The systematic imprisonment
of young people of color

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:19.046
that started in this era

00:22:19.046 --> 00:22:20.940
effectively removed from cities

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:23.010
a significant portion of the young people

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:26.220
who had committed and sustained
the political violence.

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:28.200
As such, by the mid-1970s,

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:31.470
75% of Black people in American prisons

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.046
were under the age of 30.

00:22:34.046 --> 00:22:35.820
Although Black Americans have been

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:38.580
disproportionately incarcerated
since the Civil War,

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:40.269
until the 1970s, they amounted to

00:22:40.269 --> 00:22:43.500
about a third of the
nation's prison population.

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:46.260
This changed in the mid-1970s,
when Black, Puerto Rican,

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:48.120
and Central and South American groups

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:52.030
began to approach majorities
in state and federal prisons.

00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:54.810
The mass incarceration of
Black and Brown Americans

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:56.160
over the past half century

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:59.370
can be partly attributed to
the socioeconomic conditions

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:01.530
that lay at the root of the rebellions.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:04.080
And that became even worse
as the decade wore on,

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:05.730
with the end of the War on Poverty

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.463
and the simultaneous
escalation of the War on Crime.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:13.944
The fires in Miami in 1980,
in Los Angeles in 1992,

00:23:13.944 --> 00:23:16.350
of course nationally televised moments

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:17.610
of mass political violence

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:20.130
that reached a truly unprecedented scale,

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:22.710
are legacies of the
historical trends that began

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:26.910
in the late 1960s and at the
dawn of mass incarceration.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:29.008
In both cases, people did not erupt

00:23:29.008 --> 00:23:32.491
in response to a specific
incident of police brutality,

00:23:32.491 --> 00:23:35.811
but instead to groups of police
officers who were acquitted

00:23:35.811 --> 00:23:38.940
despite undeniable evidence of abuse.

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:42.000
In the LA case, the
89-second video of officers

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.610
striking Rodney King with their
feet and their billy clubs

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:46.740
between 53 and 56 times,

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:48.238
which of course was

00:23:48.238 --> 00:23:49.740
the first viral video of police brutality

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:52.230
and the 'proof' of police brutality

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:54.720
in the white public imagination, right?

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:58.590
Did not move the jury in
Simi Valley to convict.

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:00.471
By the time LA exploded,

00:24:00.471 --> 00:24:04.200
the prison population had
exploded more than fivefold

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:06.780
from fewer than 200,000 people in 1970

00:24:06.780 --> 00:24:09.720
to 1.1 million in 1990.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:11.190
And it would continue to explode

00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:14.040
during the Clinton administration,
with the '94 crime bill

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:16.950
cementing the United States
as the country defined

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:20.160
in part, at least, by mass incarceration.

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:23.340
The violence in Cincinnati
in 2001, Ferguson, Baltimore,

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.740
and a handful of other
cities during the Obama years

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:28.766
and in the summer of 2020
occurred in reaction to

00:24:28.766 --> 00:24:33.060
exceptional incidents of police
violence or police killings.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:34.407
There are no longer rebellions

00:24:34.407 --> 00:24:36.570
against everyday policing practices,

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:39.930
against arrests and the
specter of incarceration,

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:42.420
a sign that the status
quo has become accepted

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:45.120
in many over-policed and
under-protected communities,

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.406
however bitterly.

00:24:46.406 --> 00:24:48.120
And in this sense at least,

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:51.087
national and local authorities
won the War on Crime.

00:24:51.087 --> 00:24:53.310
And yet, as long as the
criminal legal system

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:55.950
proves time and time again to function as

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:58.950
a means to lock people of
color and poor people away

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:00.300
for long prison terms

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:02.130
and to sanction police violence,

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:04.380
rebellion remains a possibility.

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:06.284
I'll end there. Thank you.

00:25:06.284 --> 00:25:09.451
(audience applauding)

00:25:16.146 --> 00:25:18.162
(Ekow clearing throat)
- Excuse me.

00:25:18.162 --> 00:25:20.340
Let me join the course in
thanking the organizers

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:23.866
and our host of this terrific conference.

00:25:23.866 --> 00:25:26.497
I was speaking to Peter
yesterday and he said,

00:25:26.497 --> 00:25:30.606
"Oh, it's a great group,
this thing will run itself."

00:25:30.606 --> 00:25:33.212
And I just pointed out to him that

00:25:33.212 --> 00:25:35.237
that's the kind of thing that great hosts,

00:25:35.237 --> 00:25:36.870
whether it be dinner parties
or conferences, always say.

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:39.300
They work incredibly
hard behind the scenes

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:41.190
to make things look like
they run themselves,

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:42.740
but they most certainly do not.

00:25:43.691 --> 00:25:45.960
And we are grateful for all your work.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:49.287
Let me also take a moment to
thank you all for being here.

00:25:49.287 --> 00:25:53.119
You know, my students have to
sit in rooms and listen to me.

00:25:53.119 --> 00:25:58.100
So the the voluntary engagement
is very deeply appreciated.

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:02.310
One of the things that's
great about seeing,

00:26:02.310 --> 00:26:03.723
oh, let me turn on my timer.

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:06.713
Forgive me.

00:26:07.662 --> 00:26:09.840
As an academic, I really
ought to turn on a timer

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:11.223
lest we be here all day.

00:26:12.487 --> 00:26:17.220
One of the great things
about such a group gathering

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:20.490
given the title is the
engagement and belief that

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:22.500
the public imagination matters greatly

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:26.010
when we think and talk
about mass incarceration.

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:30.270
I think that need not be
too long litigated here.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:31.669
That is to say the public
imagination, and in particular

00:26:31.669 --> 00:26:36.090
the public imagination when
it comes to race and crime,

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:38.009
I think is deeply important,

00:26:38.009 --> 00:26:39.690
or one might say when
it comes to race at all.

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:42.060
So from Black princes to Black princesses,

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:44.940
whether it be the Black
Panther or "The Little Mermaid"

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:47.700
to secular kings like the Obamas,

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:49.530
it is striking to see how much

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:51.660
images of Blackness in the public media

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:54.990
stir instant passion, anger, and upset.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:59.010
And I think this is in no
place more powerful than

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:02.940
when it comes to the way in
which race and crime are linked.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:06.850
So my core idea starts where
many of you have already known

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:08.400
and where many of my
interlocutors on this panel,

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:13.400
in particular to the two people
on our ends, three people,

00:27:13.552 --> 00:27:15.390
have engaged with for years,

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:17.610
that is the by now well-known idea that

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:21.120
one of the problems in our
American imagination is

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.550
the fusing of criminality and race.

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:25.410
That is in the very idea that

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:27.593
a Black man is presumptively criminal.

00:27:27.593 --> 00:27:30.240
That work has been so deeply explored.

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.240
I actually don't wanna
litigate it much here.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:34.950
For those of you who are more interested

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:37.257
or newer to that idea,
I'm happy to recommend

00:27:37.257 --> 00:27:41.223
what are now countless really
thoughtful pieces on it.

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:45.900
What I want to instead
speak about is the idea that

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:50.820
how violence is visited on
Black men is in connection to

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:52.500
Black men being presumptively criminal

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:54.510
considered a form of policing.

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:56.143
And that's true,

00:27:56.143 --> 00:27:58.200
whether it be by public
or private individuals.

00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:00.900
That is to say, the most
provocative way I put it is that

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:03.330
embedded in so much of white history is

00:28:03.330 --> 00:28:06.840
a kind of innate view of white privilege

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:09.240
as innate deputizing.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:11.180
So, this is a set of ideas

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:12.510
that I've been working on for a while.

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:15.180
And indeed, I have to
say, the word "deputizing"

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:17.100
came to me recently at a conference

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:21.120
actually that we were
sharing, and I hoped that it,

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:24.120
indeed, one of the things
I want to test with you

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:28.830
is whether it captures the
idea about which I'm trying,

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:31.261
the idea I'm trying to pin down.

00:28:31.261 --> 00:28:33.547
So, what do I mean?

00:28:33.547 --> 00:28:37.500
"Deputizing" here is meant to
specify a form of policing,

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:39.780
and that is to say,
deputization highlights

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:43.170
a particular relationship
between policing and violence.

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:45.150
Here, unlike Tracy Meares's work,

00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:46.830
which recently has started exploring

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:49.200
the wider use of our term "policing,"

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:52.080
for those of you in the law
schools here or elsewhere,

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:53.670
the term "police power" is

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.952
a deep and long historic legal term

00:28:55.952 --> 00:28:57.600
with the broadest of uses.

00:28:57.600 --> 00:28:58.473
That is to say,

00:28:59.478 --> 00:29:01.170
it is as much part of the
police powers of New Haven

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:02.490
that they have zoning laws

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:04.840
as it is that they have
actual police officers.

00:29:05.752 --> 00:29:09.030
But it is also true that the
same breath refracts through

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:11.853
our moral and colloquial thinking as well.

00:29:12.766 --> 00:29:14.755
So we might talk about policing each other

00:29:14.755 --> 00:29:15.588
in the way we behave.

00:29:15.588 --> 00:29:18.030
You know, we might say
"I police my children."

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:20.550
It is as far as we know, my main job.

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:22.740
We might say we police
each other's language,

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:23.853
things like that.

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:26.910
And I don't wanna deny
that policing itself,

00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:29.023
that is, who is allowed to police who,

00:29:29.023 --> 00:29:31.680
instantly invites hierarchy.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:34.560
So for example, if Tracy
Meares and I are having a fight

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:38.220
over whether or not I should
have cited some scholar, right?

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:39.847
Whenever we're policing academic norms,

00:29:39.847 --> 00:29:42.360
it is going to to be
contested whether or not

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:44.587
a student can suddenly pipe up and say,

00:29:44.587 --> 00:29:46.980
"Professor Yankah, you
should have" dot dot dot.

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:48.630
One might say, "No, no, no,

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:50.550
Professor Meares and I
are having that debate."

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:51.837
That is to say,

00:29:51.837 --> 00:29:55.024
there's hierarchy in
policing that is understood.

00:29:55.024 --> 00:29:56.839
But what I wanna do is

00:29:56.839 --> 00:29:58.110
focus on policing in a more specific way.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:03.110
That is to say, policing
is not just about norms,

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:06.840
but in this case I wanna talk
about policing as a form of

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:10.828
justified deployment of norm
guarding and sanctioning.

00:30:10.828 --> 00:30:12.273
Fair enough.

00:30:12.273 --> 00:30:16.247
But that when I speak about
policing and deputization,

00:30:16.247 --> 00:30:20.310
I'm arguing that violence and policing are

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:22.980
in this sense deeply connected, right?

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:25.146
So I'm not just talking
about the way we might

00:30:25.146 --> 00:30:26.850
police each other in our
normal everyday behavior.

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:27.900
Deputization is about

00:30:28.768 --> 00:30:30.150
the way we police each
other through violence.

00:30:30.150 --> 00:30:32.580
And because violence
is especially salient,

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:35.981
deputization, the argument
goes, has its own expression.

00:30:35.981 --> 00:30:37.740
So instead, I wanna preserve

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:39.690
the salience of potential violence,

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:41.404
but I also wanna disconnect it from

00:30:41.404 --> 00:30:45.570
formally authorized policing by the state.

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:47.150
And that's what I mean by deputization.

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:50.130
So, so far, everybody with me?

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:51.337
All right.

00:30:51.337 --> 00:30:54.210
So, deputization in this sense

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:55.860
has existed for a long, long time.

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:57.709
For those of you who
are policing scholars,

00:30:57.709 --> 00:31:00.237
actually, better yet, for those of you

00:31:00.237 --> 00:31:02.670
who are not policing scholars,

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:04.050
it's always hard for us to remember that

00:31:04.050 --> 00:31:06.030
policing is actually
kind of a new phenomenon.

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:09.330
That is, modern policing
is only, I don't know,

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:11.220
160 years or something like this.

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:13.606
- [Panelist] 1867.

00:31:13.606 --> 00:31:16.590
- So that's Peel's London, right?

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:18.747
So 18, 140-

00:31:18.747 --> 00:31:19.903
- [Panelist] Here.

00:31:19.903 --> 00:31:20.736
- Here?

00:31:20.736 --> 00:31:21.885
- [Panelist] Yeah, Peel's (indistinct).

00:31:21.885 --> 00:31:22.877
- I should do the math.

00:31:22.877 --> 00:31:24.090
You get the idea.

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:25.530
One of the reasons I
bring this up actually is

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:27.720
when I teach philosophy 101,

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:29.537
when I rarely get to teach philosophy 101,

00:31:29.537 --> 00:31:32.820
one of the interesting things
about philosophy is that

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:34.440
a bunch of things we teach students

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:37.020
are so deeply captured
in our moments, right?

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:38.366
So we say things like

00:31:38.366 --> 00:31:41.115
I don't know, let me
give you a silly example.

00:31:41.115 --> 00:31:45.024
The sign of a just and
authorized state power is

00:31:45.024 --> 00:31:47.370
monopoly over its borders, right?

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:49.710
If you would go back 200 years ago,

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:51.180
that would've been a fanciful idea.

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:53.040
The state just didn't have the power.

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:56.130
I mean, there just weren't the
technologies that we have now

00:31:56.130 --> 00:31:57.090
that you could.

00:31:57.090 --> 00:31:59.200
So too with policing.

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:00.399
When we talk about the fact that

00:32:00.399 --> 00:32:01.232
a justified state has police,

00:32:01.232 --> 00:32:02.910
it's actually quite stunning
for us to remember that

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:05.850
police were quite a modern invention.

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:06.780
Why do I say this?

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.070
The point is policing,

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:11.130
if you go back only a
century and a half ago,

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:13.560
was often done by what
we call "human cry,"

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:16.410
that is, the justified constable, right?

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:21.180
The police officer would,
the official idea was,

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:24.487
if somebody was a felon,
caught in the act of a felon,

00:32:24.487 --> 00:32:26.700
could "raise human cry."

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:28.537
And that would just to be simply to say to

00:32:28.537 --> 00:32:30.771
"all law-abiding citizens,
you are now deputies,

00:32:30.771 --> 00:32:33.330
come with me and let's
capture this felon," right?

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:35.550
So deputization is a quite powerful way

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:40.230
in which policing has always occurred.

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:43.890
So too, obviously it has
its links in Jim Crow South

00:32:43.890 --> 00:32:46.440
where white people could
be deputized at any time

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:50.970
in order to enforce the law
and violence against Negroes,

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:53.910
especially escaped slaves
before the Jim Crow period

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:57.213
and unruly Negroes afterwards, right?

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:02.245
More importantly, I wanna
point out that deputization was

00:33:02.245 --> 00:33:06.030
about that feeling continuing
through our history,

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:10.410
the license that white people
had to feel or got to feel

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:12.600
about their ability to enforce violence,

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:15.423
both formal and informal, to
keep Black people in place.

00:33:15.423 --> 00:33:18.510
And that's true again and
again, even in less formal ways,

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:20.010
in large swaths of the country.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:21.330
So I'm thinking, for example,

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:23.700
during natural disasters
and times of threat

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:26.160
when many white communities
simply armed themselves

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:30.450
to police areas with a
firm understanding that

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:33.420
their activities were
not illegal vigilantism,

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:35.700
but something like deputization.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:38.250
Here then, this of course
was often done with

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:40.711
the tacit approval of
formal police, right?

00:33:40.711 --> 00:33:44.100
But here then is why I
want to make the claim that

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:45.960
deputization is important.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:48.052
This is a difference between

00:33:48.052 --> 00:33:50.710
a kind of mindset about deputization

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:54.300
and the kind of mindset
about ordinary violence.

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:57.008
That is to say, deputization
is the idea that

00:33:57.008 --> 00:33:59.640
one is not just justified
in using violence,

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:00.960
but that one is authorized.

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:02.730
That is to say, one is, so to speak,

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:06.210
made an arm of the state
when one does this.

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:08.940
So the examples of course
are countless, but think of

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:11.430
George Zimmerman who very
much deputized himself

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:13.920
as a one-man neighborhood watch.

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:16.769
Think of Ahmaud Arbery's
killers, who are complicated

00:34:16.769 --> 00:34:18.660
in that they were former police officers,

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:19.890
but when they see a Black man

00:34:19.890 --> 00:34:21.240
jogging through the neighborhood,

00:34:21.240 --> 00:34:23.010
they simply raise human cry.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:25.443
They call their friend,
they jump in a truck

00:34:25.443 --> 00:34:28.620
and decide they're going
to capture Ahmaud Arbery

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:31.470
and they experience, at
least in their reporting,

00:34:31.470 --> 00:34:33.667
his resistance to their asking to stop

00:34:33.667 --> 00:34:37.412
as his resistance to their
authorized power, right?

00:34:37.412 --> 00:34:39.687
That's a very different kind of thing that

00:34:39.687 --> 00:34:42.750
if you and I were engaged in
violence against each other,

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:45.420
to which I'll respond later
to, which I'll return later.

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:49.410
So too Kyle Rittenhouse sees
protestors in Philadelphia

00:34:49.410 --> 00:34:51.630
and he goes and gets a long gun

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:54.870
and decides that he's going
to join in his imagination,

00:34:54.870 --> 00:34:57.180
not, by the way, entirely unfounded,

00:34:57.180 --> 00:34:58.770
numbers of people who are simply going to

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:02.670
impose order on their
community through arms.

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:07.670
and, if less fatally,
Mark and Patricia McClosky

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:10.620
who are famous for having pointed guns at

00:35:10.620 --> 00:35:12.240
protestors marching by their home

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:15.300
again, because they felt
they were authorized to.

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:17.562
So what do I wanna say about deputization?

00:35:17.562 --> 00:35:18.720
Two points.

00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:20.986
One is that, in this sense,

00:35:20.986 --> 00:35:23.910
deputization is an innate part
of our white culture, right?

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:25.920
It is the power to think of yourself as

00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:29.220
authorized to dispense violence
as against Black people.

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:31.560
Not in a way that does wrong,

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:34.320
but in a way that makes
you an arm of the state.

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:36.150
And I wanna say that this
of course doesn't mean

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:38.100
that violence itself is a new concept.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:40.047
That would be absurd, right?

00:35:40.047 --> 00:35:43.548
But deputization is a powerful concept.

00:35:43.548 --> 00:35:47.190
If Zachary and I are engaged
in violence against each other,

00:35:47.190 --> 00:35:49.236
we understand ourselves to be engaged in

00:35:49.236 --> 00:35:53.520
extra legal behavior and
perhaps even illegal behavior.

00:35:53.520 --> 00:35:54.600
Of course, there are communities where

00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:55.923
this is more complicated.

00:35:56.828 --> 00:35:57.690
I want to make sure that's clear.

00:35:57.690 --> 00:35:58.950
There are communities where

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:00.600
the state is sufficiently failing

00:36:00.600 --> 00:36:02.670
that people have long
thought of their violence as

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:03.600
authorized by the state.

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:06.127
The mafia would be the most
obvious example, right?

00:36:06.127 --> 00:36:07.837
"Cosa nostra" meant "this thing of ours."

00:36:07.837 --> 00:36:09.810
"The state was insufficiently protective,

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:12.270
so we will authorize ourselves."

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.280
But it is quite something
to think of yourself as

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:17.820
lately empowered to do that
everywhere at any time.

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:20.400
That is to say, rather
than my thinking that

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:22.980
when I hit Zach, I'm doing something wrong

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:24.330
or something to be punished,

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:26.700
or that he and I are just even

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:28.830
dispensing cruelty against each other.

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:31.650
To think that when I hit Zach,
I'm authorized by the state,

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:33.291
that his resistance is resistance to

00:36:33.291 --> 00:36:34.812
not my justified power.

00:36:34.812 --> 00:36:37.227
It's not self defense, it's policing.

00:36:37.227 --> 00:36:39.180
It's authorized policing.

00:36:39.180 --> 00:36:44.180
And that my claim is a
powerful kind of thinking

00:36:45.484 --> 00:36:47.970
that Black people have not had access to

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:50.823
and that changes white lives,
that changes Black lives.

00:36:51.931 --> 00:36:55.950
That that is to say that Black
people live in a world often,

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.680
by the way, as an aside, I will say

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:00.000
even as I get passionate about this,

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.490
I dislike speaking so broadly.

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:04.950
It is obvious that people
have different experiences,

00:37:04.950 --> 00:37:08.070
different places in the
hierarchy of power and wealth.

00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:10.500
I don't mean to elide that,

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:13.050
but what is true is that
Black people are aware that

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:14.340
they live in a world where

00:37:14.340 --> 00:37:16.410
it's not just that violence
can be visited on them,

00:37:16.410 --> 00:37:17.910
that's true of everybody,

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:19.410
but that violence can be visited on them

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:22.750
by people who feel themselves
authorized to do so, right?

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:25.380
I usually actually don't
like to be very personal.

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:28.950
I think there's a kind of
worry that Black scholars are

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:33.060
asked to trade personality
for credibility.

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:35.921
But I will in one moment just point out

00:37:35.921 --> 00:37:40.921
when my boys touch other
people's cars, I am frightened.

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:43.260
And my boys are children.

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:45.930
I am frightened because they
will not always be children.

00:37:45.930 --> 00:37:49.350
And it is not that people
will respond to them wrongly,

00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:52.320
i.e. "don't touch my car, I'm angry."

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:54.172
It's that people will feel authorized

00:37:54.172 --> 00:37:56.280
to visit violence on them

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:58.110
because they're presumptively criminal.

00:37:58.110 --> 00:37:59.490
That is the power of deputization

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:01.953
that is different than
the power of violence.

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:06.183
That was not in my papers.

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:08.220
Okay.

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:10.653
The question with which
I wanna end is this.

00:38:12.388 --> 00:38:17.388
Well, one question which I wanna ask is,

00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:24.540
well, okay, one meta
question I want to ask is

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:27.420
does the idea of deputization add anything

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:30.030
to the idea of Black people
as presumptively criminal

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:33.750
and is this simply
racist violence as such?

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:35.880
Or does the idea of
deputization actually change

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:37.110
the way we can think about this?

00:38:37.110 --> 00:38:39.480
That's a kind of first order question.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:42.411
But setting that aside,
the other questions I

00:38:42.411 --> 00:38:43.530
oh, my timer didn't beep.

00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:44.830
Then I'll really conclude.

00:38:45.929 --> 00:38:46.762
Setting that aside, what I wanna say is

00:38:47.827 --> 00:38:49.487
I've recently been working on writings

00:38:49.487 --> 00:38:50.370
with Keith Humphries at Stanford

00:38:50.370 --> 00:38:53.820
on the changing demographics
of mass incarceration.

00:38:53.820 --> 00:38:55.320
In particular, as many of you know,

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:56.870
we're in a particular moment of

00:38:57.759 --> 00:39:00.180
kind of left-right
convergence on decarceration

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:02.964
and that has meant that the
percentage of Black prisoners

00:39:02.964 --> 00:39:05.970
is dropping relative to the
percentage of white prisoners,

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:07.620
indeed dropping faster.

00:39:07.620 --> 00:39:09.870
Part of this is kind of feature not bug,

00:39:09.870 --> 00:39:12.687
i.e. when you use your criminal law system

00:39:12.687 --> 00:39:14.130
to imprison Black people,

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:15.227
if you affect your criminal law system,

00:39:15.227 --> 00:39:16.967
you'll free Black people at a faster rate.

00:39:16.967 --> 00:39:19.950
What I want to think
about is whether or not

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:23.910
that has an effect of
undermining or destabilizing

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:26.790
the image of Blacks as
presumptively criminal.

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:29.640
And whether that itself has
the ability to undermine

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:33.360
this white innate power of deputization,

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:34.500
the ability of white people

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:37.050
to police Black people through violence.

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:38.535
What I also want to know is can

00:39:38.535 --> 00:39:40.920
if this historical image
of the Black criminal

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:43.920
can be combated to drain
the idea of legitimization,

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:46.050
what happens to the idea of deputization?

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:48.360
So for example, there is suggestive work

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:51.873
currently on for felony murder,
which I will not explain,

00:39:52.709 --> 00:39:55.110
but the way felony murder turns out

00:39:55.110 --> 00:39:57.330
is often used to punish
people disproportionately.

00:39:57.330 --> 00:39:59.070
It is often used itself,

00:39:59.070 --> 00:40:01.623
not just disproportionately
for their crime,

00:40:01.623 --> 00:40:02.580
but it's also often used in

00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:05.280
vastly racially
disproportionate ways, right?

00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:09.060
So Black people are prosecuted
for felony murder one

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:13.134
in crimes that white people
would not be prosecuted for.

00:40:13.134 --> 00:40:16.343
Interesting work has
been recently done where

00:40:16.343 --> 00:40:19.050
audit studies have been
done trying to sort out

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:21.000
white names, Black
names, and Hispanic names

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:24.510
to see what potential jurors would do.

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:28.170
And interestingly, while
white jurors were punished

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:31.140
less under felony murder statutes,

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:33.184
Black jurors actually weren't
punished that much more

00:40:33.184 --> 00:40:37.530
when jurors were made alert
that these were Black jurors.

00:40:37.530 --> 00:40:39.540
That is to say, they may
be guarding themselves

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:41.662
in this new era of thinking

00:40:41.662 --> 00:40:44.280
about race and mass incarceration.

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:45.750
But Hispanic jurors were punished

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:47.880
vastly out of disproportion.

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:52.620
So the worry is can we undermine
racial thinking about crime

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:56.190
sufficient to undermine deputization?

00:40:56.190 --> 00:40:57.480
Or the worry might be

00:40:57.480 --> 00:41:00.420
do we simply push and
hide it into other spaces

00:41:00.420 --> 00:41:01.980
where our guards aren't up?

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:04.140
And these are the ideas that
I'm starting to wrest with.

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:05.649
I will stop there.

00:41:05.649 --> 00:41:08.816
(audience applauding)

00:41:10.229 --> 00:41:12.330
- [Judith] I think we're
standing as you like.

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:13.580
- I'm gonna sit, I think.

00:41:14.882 --> 00:41:16.246
I'm gonna see.

00:41:16.246 --> 00:41:17.607
- [Panelist] I think it works.

00:41:17.607 --> 00:41:19.065
- Does it work?

00:41:19.065 --> 00:41:20.147
- [Panelist] It works.

00:41:20.147 --> 00:41:21.186
- Now I've got two.

00:41:21.186 --> 00:41:22.602
- [Ekow] Take 'em both.

00:41:22.602 --> 00:41:23.638
(Ekow chuckling)

00:41:23.638 --> 00:41:24.827
(panelists murmuring)

00:41:24.827 --> 00:41:28.244
- I'll take Ekow's, I know Ekow's worked.

00:41:29.158 --> 00:41:30.570
Let's see here.

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:32.220
And I would like to echo Ekow's thoughts

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:35.070
about thanking everybody and
thanking you for being here.

00:41:36.037 --> 00:41:37.497
Can you hear me all right?

00:41:37.497 --> 00:41:39.259
- [Judith] A little louder.

00:41:39.259 --> 00:41:40.092
- Okay.

00:41:40.092 --> 00:41:40.925
I'll try to be a little louder.

00:41:41.957 --> 00:41:43.470
I'm very interested in what Ekow just said

00:41:44.372 --> 00:41:46.043
about "deputization,"
the word "deputization"

00:41:47.659 --> 00:41:49.740
and I think one of the
things you asked is if

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:51.810
feedback was positive or
negative about the word.

00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:56.490
And I think it's a very
potent one word way to suggest

00:41:56.490 --> 00:41:59.820
a lot of things that
we're talking about today.

00:41:59.820 --> 00:42:02.547
And I wanna go from there
to loop back to some of

00:42:02.547 --> 00:42:05.520
the things we were talking
about in the first session,

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:09.302
which had to do I think
with the imagination,

00:42:09.302 --> 00:42:11.640
the way incarcerated people are imagined

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:16.640
and in particular what
fiction has to say about that.

00:42:17.492 --> 00:42:21.690
And if we think about some of
what Ekow just talked about,

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:23.400
I think we're talking about something that

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:25.410
Bernard was talking about
earlier, which is the way that

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:29.949
everything becomes fiction
if you look at it clearly.

00:42:29.949 --> 00:42:32.289
The idea of deputizing yourself

00:42:32.289 --> 00:42:35.970
and viewing someone else
who is a fellow citizen

00:42:35.970 --> 00:42:38.970
as someone who you are in charge of

00:42:38.970 --> 00:42:40.260
is an act of imagination.

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:41.790
It's a negative act of imagination,

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:43.830
but it's an act of imagination.

00:42:43.830 --> 00:42:47.850
So I'm going to talk a
little bit about fiction,

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:52.850
which is what I do, but
I'm gonna start by, well,

00:42:55.113 --> 00:42:58.293
I'll just read my presentation
is what I think I'll do.

00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:00.840
All right.

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:04.860
My friend, the university
sociologist Marcus Kerr,

00:43:04.860 --> 00:43:08.254
recently launched a website
called the Visiting Room.

00:43:08.254 --> 00:43:11.280
The Visiting Room compiles
videotaped interviews

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:13.170
he's done over the course of five years

00:43:13.170 --> 00:43:16.593
with men serving life at
Angola Prison in Louisiana.

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:21.270
Each of the videos is
vividly unique and intimate.

00:43:21.270 --> 00:43:24.540
When I posted the site's
link on my Facebook page,

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:26.283
one of my friends responded.

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:29.340
I just started looking at things,

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:31.193
but anyone who watches would see how

00:43:31.193 --> 00:43:34.770
basically normal and human these men are.

00:43:34.770 --> 00:43:36.900
And no possibility of parole.

00:43:36.900 --> 00:43:39.243
That's insane. That's evil.

00:43:40.190 --> 00:43:43.307
It's likely that some of my
other friends who saw my post

00:43:43.307 --> 00:43:45.790
had firsthand experience of prison

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:49.620
and so might not be surprised
by the men's stories.

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:53.430
Of course, to understand the
men serving life in prison

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:56.127
are basically normal should
not be surprising anyway.

00:43:56.127 --> 00:43:57.805
But for other people,

00:43:57.805 --> 00:44:00.728
prison has appeared as an imaginary space,

00:44:00.728 --> 00:44:05.583
a kind of blankness covered
over received ideas and images.

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:09.731
I'm (indistinct) as a novelist,

00:44:09.731 --> 00:44:12.037
and have been interested for
a long time in the question

00:44:12.037 --> 00:44:14.370
"What can fiction possibly do to address

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:16.743
this problem of misrepresention.

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:20.963
We're a long way from "Uncle Tom's Cabin"

00:44:22.113 --> 00:44:25.560
or Tugenev's "A Sportsman's Sketches,"

00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:27.661
which played important roles in

00:44:27.661 --> 00:44:31.349
the movements to abolish
slavery and suffering.

00:44:31.349 --> 00:44:35.486
Fiction is no longer in a
position to shift public policy.

00:44:35.486 --> 00:44:37.839
Perhaps what it can still do, then,

00:44:37.839 --> 00:44:40.320
is at least attempt to
create better images,

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:42.027
in the way of "The Visiting Room."

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:46.477
I'd like to read a passage from my novel

00:44:46.477 --> 00:44:49.269
"The Apartment on Calle Uruguay"

00:44:49.269 --> 00:44:51.023
and then talk a little bit about it.

00:44:52.549 --> 00:44:54.360
(indistinct) Christopher Bell is

00:44:54.360 --> 00:44:57.180
going to visit the brother
of his deceased partner

00:44:57.180 --> 00:45:00.153
at a prison that is like
Angola in Louisiana.

00:45:01.082 --> 00:45:03.945
The brother, Jesse, has a life sentence,

00:45:03.945 --> 00:45:06.916
but has received a favorable
parole recommendation

00:45:06.916 --> 00:45:09.900
and has a chance of getting
out in a year and a half.

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:11.613
So he's in this sort of a limbo.

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:15.360
Before the visit to the prison,

00:45:15.360 --> 00:45:17.970
Christopher Bell was attacked
by one of his neighbors

00:45:17.970 --> 00:45:19.470
just as he was planning to leave

00:45:19.470 --> 00:45:21.720
the United States for Mexico.

00:45:21.720 --> 00:45:22.830
He's a painter.

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:26.520
He gets assaulted by this kid
who happens to slash his eye,

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:29.470
which is an important part of
the body if you're a painter.

00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:32.870
All right, I'll just
read a little bit now.

00:45:34.777 --> 00:45:37.549
"Jesse sat by himself
at a table by the wall

00:45:37.549 --> 00:45:40.710
wearing the jeans and white
t-shirt of a 'convict'

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:42.649
as he always said,

00:45:42.649 --> 00:45:44.989
not the street clothes he normally wore.

00:45:44.989 --> 00:45:46.691
The moment I saw him,

00:45:46.691 --> 00:45:48.645
I wasn't even sure it was him at first.

00:45:48.645 --> 00:45:50.107
And when he stood up,

00:45:50.107 --> 00:45:52.440
I realized I'd signaled this uncertainty.

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:53.867
He had lost weight,

00:45:53.867 --> 00:45:57.546
his hair a little thin on
top, gray strands in it.

00:45:57.546 --> 00:45:58.470
But his eyes were so (indistinct)

00:45:58.470 --> 00:46:00.660
that he looked younger than before,

00:46:00.660 --> 00:46:03.829
younger and also simultaneously as if

00:46:03.829 --> 00:46:06.570
even the aging process got distorted

00:46:06.570 --> 00:46:09.270
by the climate of where he lived.

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:12.547
He asked how my drive had
been and I told him "fine."

00:46:12.547 --> 00:46:15.510
(indistinct) with his
old-fashioned solicitude,

00:46:15.510 --> 00:46:17.880
which meant he wasn't just asking.

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:20.457
He'd considered the
risks of the long drive

00:46:20.457 --> 00:46:22.633
and was glad I'd made it safely.

00:46:22.633 --> 00:46:26.280
I told him I was going meet
Ana in a couple of days

00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:29.250
in Texas, a state I'd never seen,

00:46:29.250 --> 00:46:32.940
and he told me the state of
Texas always brought to mind

00:46:32.940 --> 00:46:35.940
the life he wanted to live
when he got out of here.

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:39.030
A pickup truck, football on TV,

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:41.270
hunting or fishing on the weekend,

00:46:41.270 --> 00:46:43.603
a farm maybe, but maybe not.

00:46:44.469 --> 00:46:46.849
He pushed his chair closer to mine

00:46:46.849 --> 00:46:49.864
and said he wanted to
look in my healed eye.

00:46:49.864 --> 00:46:51.390
He been studying an anatomy

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:53.490
since the attack in my living room.

00:46:53.490 --> 00:46:55.388
He'd told me this before.

00:46:55.388 --> 00:46:57.223
And he put his fingertip on my eyebrow now

00:46:57.223 --> 00:46:59.887
and leaned in closer.

00:46:59.887 --> 00:47:02.668
"There are six muscles
in the eyelid," he said.

00:47:02.668 --> 00:47:05.640
Just in the lid itself, not the eye,

00:47:05.640 --> 00:47:09.198
which had its own very
complicated musculature.

00:47:09.198 --> 00:47:11.880
When it blinked, the six muscles in eyelid

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:14.730
kicked into action simultaneously.

00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:18.000
Flash. 12 reflexes fired.

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:22.777
Muscle tissue, nerve
tissue, microscopic tissue.

00:47:22.777 --> 00:47:24.570
"The blade was like a backhoe

00:47:24.570 --> 00:47:27.327
coming through all that
delicate tissue," he said.

00:47:27.327 --> 00:47:30.333
All the way down to the
level of the synapses.

00:47:31.237 --> 00:47:33.542
"It's amazing it healed," I said.

00:47:33.542 --> 00:47:37.759
He moved his chair back to the
opposite side of the table.

00:47:37.759 --> 00:47:40.887
"So you live in the country now," he said.

00:47:40.887 --> 00:47:42.907
"Sold the house, quit the job."

00:47:42.907 --> 00:47:44.827
"I should have left a
long time ago," I said.

00:47:44.827 --> 00:47:46.110
"It's easy to see it now.

00:47:46.110 --> 00:47:48.597
It would've seemed
ridiculous a few years ago."

00:47:49.450 --> 00:47:53.490
I went over to the buffet in
the hall to order some food.

00:47:53.490 --> 00:47:55.330
Jesse got a double cheeseburger,

00:47:55.330 --> 00:47:58.500
a mammoth sandwich that
dripped around his fingers

00:47:58.500 --> 00:48:00.360
and was hard to eat.

00:48:00.360 --> 00:48:03.090
I had a fish sandwich that was similar.

00:48:03.090 --> 00:48:06.226
The taste of the food, the
difficulty of eating it,

00:48:06.226 --> 00:48:10.099
the solemnity of where we
were, the fact that I'd paid,

00:48:10.099 --> 00:48:12.900
we'd been trying not
think all these thoughts

00:48:12.900 --> 00:48:14.400
and both failing,

00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:17.010
thinking and eating in
the silos of ourselves.

00:48:17.010 --> 00:48:20.610
He was involved with a old
girlfriend from high school

00:48:20.610 --> 00:48:22.063
he finally told me.

00:48:22.063 --> 00:48:24.201
A woman he hadn't seen or heard from him

00:48:24.201 --> 00:48:27.630
in more than 20 years before
she contacted him last fall.

00:48:27.630 --> 00:48:30.660
She'd started writing
him letters, then emails,

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:34.688
then talking to him on the
phone and coming for visits.

00:48:34.688 --> 00:48:37.290
I knew from conversations
we'd had in the past

00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:40.528
that eople actually had
sex in this visiting shed

00:48:40.528 --> 00:48:43.410
with its gray linoleum floor

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:47.223
and stifled gray light that
made it feel underwater.

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:50.760
Sometimes they to do it right at the table

00:48:50.760 --> 00:48:53.520
with their clothes on,
in front of the guards.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:56.100
There was a way to push the
video games closer together

00:48:56.100 --> 00:48:58.653
to create a space that
was hard to be seen in.

00:48:59.580 --> 00:49:01.080
That wasn't the kind of relationship

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:03.090
Jesse described to me now, though.

00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:04.567
She was solid, he said.

00:49:04.567 --> 00:49:06.070
Down to earth.

00:49:06.070 --> 00:49:09.037
She could talk to him about
the most boring things,

00:49:09.037 --> 00:49:11.340
the people she worked with,

00:49:11.340 --> 00:49:12.840
how none of the stores carried

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:14.940
her kind of cigarettes anymore,

00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:17.670
some lipstick she found
that tasted like cake,

00:49:17.670 --> 00:49:19.630
and he would listen intently.

00:49:19.630 --> 00:49:21.900
When you compared it to
how they fought, he said,

00:49:21.900 --> 00:49:23.880
it was like the sweet spot.

00:49:23.880 --> 00:49:25.320
Most of his life consisted of

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:28.320
sleep, work call, eating, sleep,

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:31.560
listening to her talk about
some argument with her sister.

00:49:31.560 --> 00:49:34.927
Finding a way to take her side
and argue for it sincerely

00:49:34.927 --> 00:49:36.929
was like getting high.

00:49:36.929 --> 00:49:41.068
She put money on his commissary
and paid for his cell phone.

00:49:41.068 --> 00:49:43.110
That was how they talked and texted

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:46.531
and even flirted with each
other in public on Facebook.

00:49:46.531 --> 00:49:49.080
He knew that the she was living with

00:49:49.080 --> 00:49:50.100
wasn't listening to her.

00:49:50.100 --> 00:49:53.310
He said all of that daily life.

00:49:53.310 --> 00:49:54.990
If he was honest about it, he admitted

00:49:54.990 --> 00:49:57.603
he probably wouldn't listen to it either

00:49:57.603 --> 00:49:58.530
if he was the other man.

00:49:58.530 --> 00:50:01.170
He knew this, but he
couldn't really believe it

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:02.820
because he was here, not there.

00:50:02.820 --> 00:50:05.430
It started to turn into
this whole love in his head,

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:09.660
Jessie and Antonio, like he
could hear the soundtrack.

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:11.730
I worried about the contraband cell phone,

00:50:11.730 --> 00:50:13.830
but didn't say anything.

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:16.320
The institution had
special tablet computers

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:18.420
that enabled you to
check your prison email

00:50:18.420 --> 00:50:20.400
and download music files,

00:50:20.400 --> 00:50:22.800
which cost more than ordinary ones.

00:50:22.800 --> 00:50:24.780
And I had bought Jesse
one of these players

00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:27.150
and funded it every once in a while,

00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:30.060
but now they seem quaint, inadequate.

00:50:30.060 --> 00:50:32.160
He was having a love
affair with a real woman

00:50:32.160 --> 00:50:35.280
who wanted him to have a
phone and who, Jesse told me,

00:50:35.280 --> 00:50:38.340
got angry if he didn't
stay in touch with it.

00:50:38.340 --> 00:50:39.510
He said the relationship was

00:50:39.510 --> 00:50:41.760
almost as much as about arguing and strife

00:50:41.760 --> 00:50:43.800
as it was about happiness.

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:46.921
They were drawn to each
other but always forgot.

00:50:46.921 --> 00:50:48.953
They tended to argue and look
for ways to hurt each other.

00:50:50.115 --> 00:50:52.343
They must both get off
on it, he said laughing.

00:50:52.343 --> 00:50:54.690
He told her she had to
leave the other man.

00:50:54.690 --> 00:50:56.250
It was untenable.

00:50:56.250 --> 00:50:58.112
He was going to cut it off with her

00:50:58.112 --> 00:50:59.826
if she didn't get her own apartment.

00:50:59.826 --> 00:51:02.070
But actually, he was just
like that other man, he said.

00:51:02.070 --> 00:51:04.426
All threats and no follow-through.

00:51:04.426 --> 00:51:07.442
"Masochism," he said shaking his head.

00:51:07.442 --> 00:51:10.111
"Is that what this is with me and her?"

00:51:10.111 --> 00:51:12.690
"I think it's just
ordinary drama," I said.

00:51:12.690 --> 00:51:15.272
I had that kind of feeling
with your sister sometimes,

00:51:15.272 --> 00:51:16.105
with Anna.

00:51:16.105 --> 00:51:18.720
"Now it's different, I said.

00:51:18.720 --> 00:51:21.444
Different movie. He said
different soundtrack.

00:51:21.444 --> 00:51:23.820
When I tried to imagine his daily life,

00:51:23.820 --> 00:51:27.330
I would see gray, dark
hours or lurid violent ones.

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:28.742
I'm sorry.

00:51:28.742 --> 00:51:30.522
When I used to try to
imagine his daily life,

00:51:30.522 --> 00:51:33.450
I would see gray, dark
hours or lurid violent ones

00:51:33.450 --> 00:51:37.980
instead of the textures and
hues his eyes actually saw,

00:51:37.980 --> 00:51:39.600
I still couldn't see what he saw.

00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:41.910
I knew that his equanimity

00:51:41.910 --> 00:51:43.713
was something I could never fathom.

00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:45.963
Okay, I'll leave.

00:51:46.891 --> 00:51:48.833
That's it for the fiction
part, I promise you.

00:51:51.019 --> 00:51:54.120
Okay, to go back to my
friend's comment on Facebook.

00:51:54.120 --> 00:51:57.480
Men serving life in prison are
basically normal and human.

00:51:57.480 --> 00:52:01.260
My Facebook friend said
fiction tries to enliven

00:52:01.260 --> 00:52:04.470
statements like that through
mundane, trivial details

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:08.970
that can transform abstractions
like "normal" and "human"

00:52:08.970 --> 00:52:10.492
into felt realities:

00:52:10.492 --> 00:52:14.430
the anatomy of the eye, the
messiness of the cheeseburger,

00:52:14.430 --> 00:52:16.534
the contraband cell phone.

00:52:16.534 --> 00:52:19.830
Like any art form, fiction
has the potential to

00:52:19.830 --> 00:52:22.950
organize the world's
infinite chaos of particles

00:52:22.950 --> 00:52:24.720
into a pattern.

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:27.510
Though, because fiction's
medium is language,

00:52:27.510 --> 00:52:29.210
it always suggests meaning

00:52:29.210 --> 00:52:32.310
or at least the possibility of meaning.

00:52:32.310 --> 00:52:35.970
This is what the Jewish
tradition calls "Tikkun Olan,"

00:52:35.970 --> 00:52:39.450
or reconfiguring or repairing the world.

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:42.270
My character Jesse contains particles of

00:52:42.270 --> 00:52:46.027
people I know or have met,
but he is mostly imaginary,

00:52:46.027 --> 00:52:48.749
a figment that I hope is convincing

00:52:48.749 --> 00:52:52.987
and not a kind of delivery
vehicle for themes and ideas.

00:52:52.987 --> 00:52:55.260
When I heard the topic for this session,

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:57.480
the image of the prison
and the public mind,

00:52:57.480 --> 00:52:59.640
my first thought was that

00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:02.040
that image is usually distorted or false,

00:53:02.040 --> 00:53:03.750
but I was forgetting a part of the public

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:08.289
for whom prison is not an
image, but a firsthand reality.

00:53:08.289 --> 00:53:10.074
Perhaps what a novel can do is

00:53:10.074 --> 00:53:14.872
at least connect these two
publics in a single story.

00:53:14.872 --> 00:53:18.510
I'm gonna try to flesh out
another empty abstraction now,

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:21.720
which is that prison,
that place of violence,

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:25.320
has also been shown to
me to be a sacred space,

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:27.840
like all other spaces.

00:53:27.840 --> 00:53:29.583
Little, tiny bit more fiction.

00:53:30.561 --> 00:53:31.511
It'll be pain free.

00:53:32.618 --> 00:53:35.880
I think this is an email Jesse
writes to Christopher Bell

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:36.780
later in the book.

00:53:38.437 --> 00:53:41.786
"I understand why he
would wanna go to Mexico.

00:53:41.786 --> 00:53:44.048
He wrote, "It's Trump time."

00:53:44.048 --> 00:53:45.323
By the way, this book takes place.

00:53:46.730 --> 00:53:48.385
I mean go there to stay.

00:53:48.385 --> 00:53:50.127
It got me thinking about
how when I get out of here,

00:53:50.127 --> 00:53:52.291
I'll face a totally different world.

00:53:52.291 --> 00:53:55.140
When I first came to prison,
there were no cell phones,

00:53:55.140 --> 00:53:57.787
no Facebook, barely even any internet.

00:53:57.787 --> 00:53:59.370
Guys come out of this place

00:53:59.370 --> 00:54:02.130
and can't figure out how to
buy a toothbrush, I'm told.

00:54:02.130 --> 00:54:06.150
There's too many choices or
we have to order it online.

00:54:06.150 --> 00:54:08.550
A foreign country is
what I'm talking about.

00:54:08.550 --> 00:54:10.489
Like you in Mexico,

00:54:10.489 --> 00:54:13.950
I'm gonna try to keep one
foot in and one foot out.

00:54:13.950 --> 00:54:15.270
You know what I'm saying?

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:17.570
To not forget where I came from.

00:54:17.570 --> 00:54:19.650
It's like when I got to the end of

00:54:19.650 --> 00:54:21.480
the Book of Exodus in the Bible,

00:54:21.480 --> 00:54:24.120
and there's a lot of drama going on.

00:54:24.120 --> 00:54:26.790
Moses brings down the 10 Commandments.

00:54:26.790 --> 00:54:29.250
They're about to cross over
into the promised land,

00:54:29.250 --> 00:54:31.260
enemy territory.

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:34.013
It's where a war is about to set off,

00:54:34.013 --> 00:54:36.450
and then they stop everything right there.

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:38.820
And there's maybe five
chapters just telling you

00:54:38.820 --> 00:54:40.920
how they built the tabernacle,

00:54:40.920 --> 00:54:43.383
this movable temple
with the Ark inside it.

00:54:44.310 --> 00:54:46.740
What kind of architecture there was,

00:54:46.740 --> 00:54:49.020
what kind of columns they used,

00:54:49.020 --> 00:54:51.480
the different stones
and the types of wood,

00:54:51.480 --> 00:54:53.370
the ornamentation.

00:54:53.370 --> 00:54:54.810
They tell you exactly how much

00:54:54.810 --> 00:54:57.210
gold and silver and
bronze they used to build

00:54:57.210 --> 00:55:00.600
the gates and the doors and
the hinges and the altar.

00:55:00.600 --> 00:55:02.100
Part of the ornamentation was

00:55:02.100 --> 00:55:06.120
badger skins and ram skins they dyed red.

00:55:06.120 --> 00:55:09.480
I remembered all this because
I could really see it.

00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:11.520
They're trying to paint a picture,

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:13.500
make you sit with it for a long time

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:16.713
before the story moves
forward, take it in.

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:20.430
The temple is movable.

00:55:20.430 --> 00:55:22.980
They're going to take it with
them to the promised land.

00:55:22.980 --> 00:55:24.870
But what they don't realize is that

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:27.090
they're already in the promised land.

00:55:27.090 --> 00:55:30.153
The actual arrival is
just a matter of time.

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:33.090
The whole Book of Exodus just ends

00:55:33.090 --> 00:55:35.970
with this long description of the temple.

00:55:35.970 --> 00:55:38.928
This was how it looked, the end.

00:55:38.928 --> 00:55:40.740
And I was like, "What the?"

00:55:40.740 --> 00:55:43.387
And then I realized why
they did it that way.

00:55:43.387 --> 00:55:47.340
It's because it's not about
some specific piece of land.

00:55:47.340 --> 00:55:51.101
It's about this other
space that is everywhere.

00:55:51.101 --> 00:55:53.700
And I'm gonna leave it there.

00:55:53.700 --> 00:55:55.443
I wanna just say one thing now.

00:55:56.280 --> 00:55:58.770
I have a friend who published a book

00:55:58.770 --> 00:56:00.732
about a year and a half ago now.

00:56:00.732 --> 00:56:02.025
His name is Quntos.

00:56:02.025 --> 00:56:04.890
Quntos KunQuest, whose
novel is called "This Life."

00:56:04.890 --> 00:56:08.623
Quntos has a life sentence
in Angola, is still in there.

00:56:08.623 --> 00:56:11.790
And any of you who are
interested in hearing

00:56:11.790 --> 00:56:15.810
what fiction sounds like
written from the pen of

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:20.040
someone who is in prison, I
really recommend that book.

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:21.660
It's kind of a masterpiece.

00:56:21.660 --> 00:56:23.340
I really admire it.

00:56:23.340 --> 00:56:25.563
It's called "This Life"
by Quntos KunQuest.

00:56:28.111 --> 00:56:29.972
Thanks.

00:56:29.972 --> 00:56:33.139
(audience applauding)

00:56:36.392 --> 00:56:37.313
- [Judith] Commenting, Tracey.

00:56:38.805 --> 00:56:39.805
- All right.

00:56:40.949 --> 00:56:42.282
Is this working?

00:56:43.147 --> 00:56:44.040
Yeah, so since everyone else has

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:45.720
already thanked the organizers,

00:56:45.720 --> 00:56:47.130
and those of you for being here,

00:56:47.130 --> 00:56:50.040
I'm going to take the first opportunity

00:56:50.040 --> 00:56:54.611
to thank the speakers,
so thank you very much.

00:56:54.611 --> 00:56:59.611
You might think that I might
be dreading a little bit

00:56:59.730 --> 00:57:01.140
what I'm about to do

00:57:01.140 --> 00:57:03.870
since I have just heard all
of these for the first time

00:57:03.870 --> 00:57:08.730
and I now have to say something
hopefully interesting,

00:57:08.730 --> 00:57:12.030
maybe profound and helpful

00:57:12.030 --> 00:57:14.858
to those of you sitting in
the audience to think about.

00:57:14.858 --> 00:57:18.900
But I'm actually quite joyful
about this task because

00:57:18.900 --> 00:57:23.900
it was an incredible experience
to hear all of you speak.

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:28.350
And actually, having been
here for the first session,

00:57:28.350 --> 00:57:32.329
I'm also gonna attempt
to draw some connections

00:57:32.329 --> 00:57:36.240
between the first panel and this one.

00:57:36.240 --> 00:57:38.883
So here it goes.

00:57:38.883 --> 00:57:43.303
So Judith starts out by talking about

00:57:43.303 --> 00:57:46.110
incarceration and visibility.

00:57:46.110 --> 00:57:50.460
And I think using the term
visibility is an interesting one

00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:54.600
in the context of the
subject of the conference,

00:57:54.600 --> 00:57:56.430
you know, incarceration and imagination.

00:57:56.430 --> 00:58:00.030
What role does visibility play

00:58:00.030 --> 00:58:05.030
when we are engaged in the
project of imagination?

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:10.520
And so I wanna use that
frame, the idea of visibility,

00:58:10.530 --> 00:58:15.180
contrast it with the concept
of what's not secret,

00:58:15.180 --> 00:58:19.140
which is also something that Judith said,

00:58:19.140 --> 00:58:21.960
and just sort of trouble the idea that

00:58:21.960 --> 00:58:24.630
visibility and not secret are equivalent,

00:58:24.630 --> 00:58:27.540
which I think Judith sort of suggested.

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:29.040
I'm not saying that that's what you meant,

00:58:29.040 --> 00:58:32.430
that's just literally what
you said, I wrote it down.

00:58:32.430 --> 00:58:35.760
And how the juxtaposition of those terms

00:58:35.760 --> 00:58:38.662
helps us to think about
the project of imagination.

00:58:38.662 --> 00:58:40.710
So that's one frame.

00:58:40.710 --> 00:58:43.680
The second frame is something
that Zachary offered.

00:58:43.680 --> 00:58:45.337
He asked the question

00:58:45.337 --> 00:58:49.780
"What can fiction do to
address misrepresentation?"

00:58:49.780 --> 00:58:54.360
And that made me think about
the interaction between

00:58:54.360 --> 00:58:56.520
representation on the one hand

00:58:56.520 --> 00:58:58.830
and misrepresentation on the other

00:58:58.830 --> 00:59:02.567
in the context of visibility, right?

00:59:02.567 --> 00:59:07.567
I think there, what Bernard
said in the last session,

00:59:08.343 --> 00:59:13.126
his offering, or maybe
we'll call it a provocation,

00:59:13.126 --> 00:59:17.768
that we're all engaged in fiction.

00:59:17.768 --> 00:59:22.768
What does that have to do with
visibility on the one hand,

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:26.850
representation, misrepresentation,

00:59:26.850 --> 00:59:30.390
and then this project of imagination.

00:59:30.390 --> 00:59:32.520
So that's the overall frame,

00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:36.330
and I'm gonna take each
presentation in turn,

00:59:36.330 --> 00:59:38.733
hopefully still being under time.

00:59:40.350 --> 00:59:44.160
So, Elizabeth starts out
with this idea of, you know,

00:59:44.160 --> 00:59:47.970
she talks about the image of
the prison in the public mind

00:59:47.970 --> 00:59:50.670
in the context of rebellion.

00:59:50.670 --> 00:59:55.670
And then the process of
rebellions that she describes,

00:59:56.790 --> 01:00:00.900
I think would immediately
cause us to think about

01:00:00.900 --> 01:00:03.120
what are we talking
about when we say public?

01:00:03.120 --> 01:00:05.640
Obviously there are lots of publics.

01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:08.250
I mean, for Elizabeth's subjects,

01:00:08.250 --> 01:00:12.210
she's, I think, talking
about the parts of the public

01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:16.744
involved in rebellion
itself, and these kids.

01:00:16.744 --> 01:00:21.625
And one of the things that
immediately came to mind

01:00:21.625 --> 01:00:25.680
and listening to the details
that Elizabeth was offering

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:30.120
was a connection between the first session

01:00:30.120 --> 01:00:34.205
and in particular, Joy's
comments about fugitivity

01:00:34.205 --> 01:00:36.585
(indistinct)

01:00:36.585 --> 01:00:40.260
where, you know, Elizabeth gives us

01:00:40.260 --> 01:00:44.430
this incredibly vivid
picture of teenagers,

01:00:44.430 --> 01:00:46.050
I don't even wanna call them teenagers.

01:00:46.050 --> 01:00:48.819
I mean, is a 13-year-old a teenager?

01:00:48.819 --> 01:00:50.183
Like, that's a child, right?

01:00:51.547 --> 01:00:54.330
They're babies who are, in her terms,

01:00:54.330 --> 01:00:56.760
sacrificing their own freedom

01:00:56.760 --> 01:01:01.760
against the specter, for them, of imprison

01:01:02.130 --> 01:01:07.130
so that they have an
imaginary of what this is.

01:01:07.313 --> 01:01:09.660
You know, is it visible to them?

01:01:09.660 --> 01:01:11.640
What is it that they see?

01:01:11.640 --> 01:01:15.480
To borrow another phrase
that Zachary offered,

01:01:15.480 --> 01:01:18.601
Is this a movie in their head?

01:01:18.601 --> 01:01:20.700
Is something else happening?

01:01:20.700 --> 01:01:21.533
But anyway,

01:01:22.613 --> 01:01:27.613
their actual action of
sacrificing their freedom.

01:01:27.941 --> 01:01:32.941
And I'm thinking about that
next to the prison narratives

01:01:34.478 --> 01:01:36.810
that Joy was talking about

01:01:36.810 --> 01:01:40.860
that are excluded, these
narratives of fugitivity.

01:01:40.860 --> 01:01:42.780
So one question for you is just

01:01:42.780 --> 01:01:47.780
how do we think about these
kids engaged in that act

01:01:48.180 --> 01:01:50.717
in a context where

01:01:50.717 --> 01:01:55.717
in incarceration people
are doing, is it opposite?

01:01:56.910 --> 01:01:59.760
I don't even know how to
think about fugitivity

01:01:59.760 --> 01:02:01.500
next to what they are doing,

01:02:01.500 --> 01:02:06.216
but I would like invite
you to think about that

01:02:06.216 --> 01:02:07.049
and talk about that.

01:02:08.100 --> 01:02:13.100
The next thing that Elizabeth
talks about in her comment,

01:02:14.138 --> 01:02:16.350
she's talking about

01:02:16.350 --> 01:02:19.530
maybe their imagination is
about mass incarceration.

01:02:19.530 --> 01:02:24.530
And then you tell a story about
removal of many young people

01:02:24.690 --> 01:02:27.210
from neighborhoods and places where,

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:28.440
and I'm again quoting you,

01:02:28.440 --> 01:02:31.158
it's, well, maybe this is
my interpretation of it,

01:02:31.158 --> 01:02:35.027
removing and quelling activism, right?

01:02:35.027 --> 01:02:39.270
This activism, these
rebellions are quite visible,

01:02:39.270 --> 01:02:43.440
I mean visible in the
regular, normal, everyday way

01:02:43.440 --> 01:02:45.480
we think of visibility.

01:02:45.480 --> 01:02:47.670
It's not an imagination, right?

01:02:47.670 --> 01:02:48.750
We see it.

01:02:48.750 --> 01:02:52.680
And if this quelling is removing people

01:02:52.680 --> 01:02:54.600
and putting them in prison,

01:02:54.600 --> 01:02:59.600
prison that Judith tells us is not secret,

01:02:59.672 --> 01:03:03.180
but it's also not visible,

01:03:03.180 --> 01:03:06.570
certainly not in the same
way that rebellions are,

01:03:06.570 --> 01:03:09.442
I think there's a way in
which the incarceration,

01:03:09.442 --> 01:03:13.470
even if people are still
engaged in activism,

01:03:13.470 --> 01:03:16.560
it's obscuring the nature of the activism.

01:03:16.560 --> 01:03:20.640
I wonder if that's the link to fugitivity.

01:03:20.640 --> 01:03:24.303
I don't know, but maybe
that's a jumping-off place.

01:03:25.857 --> 01:03:30.600
And then the final
thing that's I wanna say

01:03:30.600 --> 01:03:32.610
in connection with the first panel

01:03:32.610 --> 01:03:37.135
is that there's a way in
which the mass aggregates,

01:03:37.135 --> 01:03:42.135
they are not secret, not visible,

01:03:42.600 --> 01:03:47.600
but to make visible and
legible that kind of activity

01:03:48.540 --> 01:03:53.540
might require some kind of individuation.

01:03:53.780 --> 01:03:56.760
Which, you know, those of us on this panel

01:03:56.760 --> 01:03:58.980
were invited by Zachary to go to

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:01.260
the website that he spoke about,

01:04:01.260 --> 01:04:04.080
which was incredibly compelling
for me to read these,

01:04:04.080 --> 01:04:05.700
listen to these individual stories.

01:04:05.700 --> 01:04:08.740
And then I sat through the
first panel and then I thought,

01:04:08.740 --> 01:04:13.617
"Huh, am I supposed to think
about these individual stories

01:04:13.617 --> 01:04:17.400
in the same way, as
compelling in the same way

01:04:17.400 --> 01:04:22.400
given, you know, the
comments that Caleb offered."

01:04:22.890 --> 01:04:25.080
I'm just not sure.

01:04:25.080 --> 01:04:28.395
So, you know, question in there somewhere.

01:04:28.395 --> 01:04:33.270
Okay, for Ekow, he is giving us

01:04:33.270 --> 01:04:37.830
this incredibly evocative
concept of deputization.

01:04:37.830 --> 01:04:39.901
And I wish I could remember

01:04:39.901 --> 01:04:43.980
the name of the philosophy
student at offer at Harvard

01:04:43.980 --> 01:04:46.043
who actually brought this up

01:04:46.043 --> 01:04:48.180
at this conference that
Ekow and I were at.

01:04:48.180 --> 01:04:53.180
It was really jarring and also generative.

01:04:53.991 --> 01:04:58.215
I think it's a really helpful concept,

01:04:58.215 --> 01:05:01.923
but here's a thing to think about, right?

01:05:03.055 --> 01:05:06.218
You note the human cry,

01:05:06.218 --> 01:05:10.440
which creates a concept of deputization,

01:05:10.440 --> 01:05:14.730
which is in a sense
pre-state or pre-modern state

01:05:14.730 --> 01:05:16.593
as we understand it, right?

01:05:17.590 --> 01:05:19.092
It's not regulatory.

01:05:19.092 --> 01:05:22.170
And here I'm connecting with
the talk that I just gave

01:05:22.170 --> 01:05:27.170
and it's certainly pre-regulated
police power in the US

01:05:29.130 --> 01:05:31.530
as we think about it.

01:05:31.530 --> 01:05:34.317
Maybe not the continental version.

01:05:34.317 --> 01:05:38.010
So we go from deputization to, you know,

01:05:38.010 --> 01:05:40.920
here's a timeline, state creation,

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:43.680
if we're gonna call the
beginning of the modern state

01:05:43.680 --> 01:05:46.455
to use Bill Novak's formulation sort of.

01:05:46.455 --> 01:05:51.455
The New Deal, so now we're
in like the 1930s, 1940s.

01:05:52.350 --> 01:05:55.737
You say, "well of course
it's not that deputization

01:05:55.737 --> 01:06:00.737
goes away, it just becomes less visible."

01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:03.270
So there's that frame again, right?

01:06:03.270 --> 01:06:08.270
It's that it's not secret or is it secret?

01:06:08.495 --> 01:06:10.380
How do we think about its visibility?

01:06:10.380 --> 01:06:13.920
And to the extent that
it is visible today,

01:06:13.920 --> 01:06:18.750
of course it's not the
human cry so much as it's,

01:06:18.750 --> 01:06:21.720
do I wanna call it an
implicit deputization?

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:26.720
So, you know, 'Permit Patty'
by calling on the police

01:06:31.200 --> 01:06:35.730
to police activity of folks,

01:06:35.730 --> 01:06:39.390
you know, the Black
minister who is very kindly

01:06:39.390 --> 01:06:42.030
watering his neighbor's garden

01:06:42.030 --> 01:06:45.240
when the neighbor is on vacation,

01:06:45.240 --> 01:06:48.450
and when police are called, in that case,

01:06:48.450 --> 01:06:50.040
the person calling the police,

01:06:50.040 --> 01:06:53.220
is that an implicit deputation

01:06:53.220 --> 01:06:55.320
that fits in with your model?

01:06:55.320 --> 01:06:59.880
Or is that pushing your concept too far?

01:06:59.880 --> 01:07:02.797
Because I think one
answer you might have is,

01:07:02.797 --> 01:07:06.960
"well, I'm actually talking
about their own use of violence

01:07:06.960 --> 01:07:08.670
and not calling upon the state."

01:07:08.670 --> 01:07:11.760
But then how do we think about that line?

01:07:11.760 --> 01:07:14.838
Again, I think the ways in
which we think about line

01:07:14.838 --> 01:07:18.445
depends on ideas of visibility

01:07:18.445 --> 01:07:22.110
and how we think about
what visibility means.

01:07:22.110 --> 01:07:24.120
Is it just something that we know

01:07:24.120 --> 01:07:26.580
or is it something that we need to see?

01:07:26.580 --> 01:07:29.970
Is it the movie in our mind, right?

01:07:29.970 --> 01:07:34.664
And then for you, Zachary,
what I thought was fascinating

01:07:34.664 --> 01:07:38.330
about the fiction you shared

01:07:38.330 --> 01:07:43.330
was how vivid the quotidian was.

01:07:44.700 --> 01:07:47.220
So, right, you know,

01:07:47.220 --> 01:07:49.980
I just became a
pescatarian and I'm hungry,

01:07:49.980 --> 01:07:52.350
So when you started talking
about the fish sandwich,

01:07:52.350 --> 01:07:55.080
I was like, you know, "Where
is that fish sandwich?"

01:07:55.080 --> 01:07:58.312
I could kind of smell it actually.

01:07:58.312 --> 01:08:03.312
And so that starts to
bring in other senses.

01:08:03.611 --> 01:08:06.060
So when we're talking about visibility,

01:08:06.060 --> 01:08:08.520
are we talking about
something that we see?

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:10.830
Are we talking about other senses?

01:08:10.830 --> 01:08:13.367
Which brought to mind for me

01:08:13.367 --> 01:08:16.530
this book I just just picked
up, I haven't read it yet,

01:08:16.530 --> 01:08:20.580
but it's by a journalist
whose name is Ed Yong

01:08:20.580 --> 01:08:22.356
and it's something like

01:08:22.356 --> 01:08:24.867
"The Immense World of Animal Senses."

01:08:24.867 --> 01:08:29.867
And so he talks about all of
the things that humans miss

01:08:31.020 --> 01:08:36.020
because we can't see
UV light and you know,

01:08:36.360 --> 01:08:40.650
our hearing is not as keen as my puppies.

01:08:40.650 --> 01:08:44.640
And he talks about
these insects and plants

01:08:44.640 --> 01:08:47.790
that vibrate on plant leaves,

01:08:47.790 --> 01:08:51.540
and if you could actually
translate it into sound,

01:08:51.540 --> 01:08:53.550
you could actually hear a symphony.

01:08:53.550 --> 01:08:55.830
But you know, all the little bugs I guess

01:08:55.830 --> 01:08:58.451
are feeling in their feet
or something, I don't know.

01:08:58.451 --> 01:08:59.640
I don't know exactly how it works.

01:08:59.640 --> 01:09:02.220
Like I said, haven't
finished the book yet.

01:09:02.220 --> 01:09:04.592
But you know, the question is

01:09:04.592 --> 01:09:08.130
when we're trying to
come to conclusions about

01:09:08.130 --> 01:09:11.070
the role of visibility in imagination,

01:09:11.070 --> 01:09:13.890
would you maybe make an argument that

01:09:13.890 --> 01:09:18.660
what fiction does in the vivid quotidian

01:09:18.660 --> 01:09:23.660
is to allow us to push the
boundaries of our senses

01:09:23.970 --> 01:09:27.390
and then that's what's really
required for imagination?

01:09:27.390 --> 01:09:31.770
And if so, then visibility,

01:09:31.770 --> 01:09:35.580
just to start to come
back to where I began,

01:09:35.580 --> 01:09:38.613
might not be the word we want to use.

01:09:39.780 --> 01:09:40.743
That's it. Thanks.

01:09:41.588 --> 01:09:45.210
(audience applauding)

01:09:45.210 --> 01:09:47.070
- [Judith] We have
exchanges among ourselves,

01:09:47.070 --> 01:09:51.973
but we want to start by inviting
any comments or questions.

01:09:51.973 --> 01:09:54.687
Caleb, we'll start with you.

01:09:54.687 --> 01:09:55.950
And please introduce yourself,

01:09:55.950 --> 01:09:57.840
and I think you need
to scream a little bit.

01:09:57.840 --> 01:09:59.617
Oh, microphones are coming to you.

01:10:00.634 --> 01:10:03.306
So just reintroduce yourself as well.

01:10:03.306 --> 01:10:04.440
- Hi, Caleb Smith.

01:10:04.440 --> 01:10:08.310
Thanks for this really illuminating panel.

01:10:08.310 --> 01:10:09.460
I really appreciate it.

01:10:10.345 --> 01:10:13.823
I wanted to pick up, Tracey,
on something you said about,

01:10:13.823 --> 01:10:16.620
you and I can pick up
the individuation thing,

01:10:16.620 --> 01:10:19.737
but really about Ekow's paper

01:10:19.737 --> 01:10:21.570
and the concept of deputization,

01:10:21.570 --> 01:10:24.834
which I found really valuable

01:10:24.834 --> 01:10:29.130
and I do think this is
one of the places where

01:10:29.130 --> 01:10:31.380
intellectual work can have
some leverage actually,

01:10:31.380 --> 01:10:34.920
is by introducing concepts
that then just get picked up

01:10:34.920 --> 01:10:37.020
and they condense a lot of thinking

01:10:37.020 --> 01:10:38.850
and they have a life in the culture

01:10:38.850 --> 01:10:41.190
and they allow people to name phenomena.

01:10:41.190 --> 01:10:43.050
But like Tracey, my
specific question was about

01:10:43.050 --> 01:10:47.085
whether the state is the
authorizer of deputization.

01:10:47.085 --> 01:10:50.040
If I think about January 6th,

01:10:50.040 --> 01:10:52.492
I think about the state as a threat to

01:10:52.492 --> 01:10:56.488
the felt deputization of the people there.

01:10:56.488 --> 01:10:58.230
If I think about

01:10:58.230 --> 01:11:01.320
what people think Second
Amendment is protecting them from,

01:11:01.320 --> 01:11:05.490
it's from state encroachment
on their deputized power

01:11:05.490 --> 01:11:08.256
to do the kinds of things
that you're describing.

01:11:08.256 --> 01:11:12.516
So I wondered if, and I do think

01:11:12.516 --> 01:11:15.907
there are moments of quite
clear state authorization.

01:11:15.907 --> 01:11:20.907
"If you see something, say
something" is an example.

01:11:20.940 --> 01:11:25.380
But I wonder if something
like, to me it seems,

01:11:25.380 --> 01:11:27.810
like in some of the cases
you're talking about,

01:11:27.810 --> 01:11:30.954
it's something like 'the nation'

01:11:30.954 --> 01:11:33.690
and a kind of white nationalism

01:11:33.690 --> 01:11:36.150
that's authorizing the deputization,

01:11:36.150 --> 01:11:38.517
which can actually then be
turned against the state

01:11:38.517 --> 01:11:41.970
as being secular, multicultural,

01:11:41.970 --> 01:11:43.956
limiting on this kind of power.

01:11:43.956 --> 01:11:48.720
So, I also do think there's a kind of

01:11:48.720 --> 01:11:53.040
expectation of exoneration
that's built into it, so fair.

01:11:53.040 --> 01:11:56.092
But I'm asking, I just
wanted to think about

01:11:56.092 --> 01:12:01.092
whether there's even a
certain kind of more mystical

01:12:01.950 --> 01:12:06.950
or fascistic deputizing power
at work here than the state

01:12:07.410 --> 01:12:11.893
as that is because there's
obviously a lot of, you know,

01:12:11.893 --> 01:12:14.483
white nationalist anti-state
sentiment of its own type.

01:12:17.358 --> 01:12:19.200
- So, thank you for the question, Caleb.

01:12:19.200 --> 01:12:20.719
Thank you for the question.

01:12:20.719 --> 01:12:24.630
So this is, I mean, as many
of you know, this is actually

01:12:24.630 --> 01:12:28.950
the really great moment
when you have an idea,

01:12:28.950 --> 01:12:30.453
you think it has real shape,

01:12:31.418 --> 01:12:32.310
but you don't quite know it yet.

01:12:32.310 --> 01:12:33.420
Because after this,

01:12:33.420 --> 01:12:35.160
after a year or so of defending an idea,

01:12:35.160 --> 01:12:37.890
you're just sort of like
trying to survive, right?

01:12:37.890 --> 01:12:39.540
So this is fun because

01:12:39.540 --> 01:12:43.050
I really don't know the
answer to your questions.

01:12:43.050 --> 01:12:44.880
I'm, you know,

01:12:44.880 --> 01:12:49.069
in a way that perhaps is
insufficiently protective,

01:12:49.069 --> 01:12:51.273
just thinking with you all.

01:12:52.110 --> 01:12:54.390
But here are a couple thoughts I do have.

01:12:54.390 --> 01:12:58.590
So I take your question as
of a piece with Tracey's

01:12:58.590 --> 01:13:03.060
When does the ability to cast your hand,

01:13:03.060 --> 01:13:07.680
wave power at people become deputization,

01:13:07.680 --> 01:13:10.270
when, after all, it can
be calling the police

01:13:11.411 --> 01:13:14.154
to do something that the police, you know,

01:13:14.154 --> 01:13:16.499
to enforce essentially your social norms.

01:13:16.499 --> 01:13:18.247
"I don't want this person barbecuing."

01:13:18.247 --> 01:13:23.247
Or is it, as I was trying
to, I think, encircle,

01:13:24.480 --> 01:13:28.410
when you feel authorized
to use force yourself?

01:13:28.410 --> 01:13:32.634
So I'm gonna just, so
that I don't go on and on,

01:13:32.634 --> 01:13:34.200
I'm gonna use three examples,
say they're on a spectrum,

01:13:34.200 --> 01:13:36.030
but say I want deputization to pick out

01:13:36.030 --> 01:13:37.860
a particular end of the spectrum.

01:13:37.860 --> 01:13:40.320
So one example is self-defense, right?

01:13:40.320 --> 01:13:42.510
People feel authorized to use self-defense

01:13:42.510 --> 01:13:44.130
and use violence in self-defense.

01:13:44.130 --> 01:13:45.750
And it's a powerful authorization.

01:13:45.750 --> 01:13:47.760
Indeed, when you teach
self-defense and criminal law,

01:13:47.760 --> 01:13:49.200
one of the really interesting things is

01:13:49.200 --> 01:13:51.360
it's hard to give a
particular justification

01:13:51.360 --> 01:13:53.730
of self-defense, but nobody's
willing to give it up.

01:13:53.730 --> 01:13:57.060
It's one of the most
deeply felt moral claims

01:13:57.060 --> 01:14:00.180
that I can use violence to
protect myself as against you.

01:14:00.180 --> 01:14:01.620
But I don't think that's deputization.

01:14:01.620 --> 01:14:04.500
People feel that as a
kind of natural rights.

01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:06.254
And that's interesting to me.

01:14:06.254 --> 01:14:07.980
I said three examples,

01:14:07.980 --> 01:14:10.094
I'm gonna use four cause you gave me one.

01:14:10.094 --> 01:14:13.197
The Second Amendment I think
is something like that.

01:14:13.197 --> 01:14:14.850
It is a felt kind of natural right.

01:14:14.850 --> 01:14:17.430
That some people, at least
in the American psyche

01:14:17.430 --> 01:14:18.900
and very few other places,

01:14:18.900 --> 01:14:22.740
and I don't, I think you're
right, it is not, you know,

01:14:22.740 --> 01:14:24.540
if you were to tell most people

01:14:24.540 --> 01:14:27.377
that self-defense is a state
granted right, they'd say no.

01:14:27.377 --> 01:14:29.550
To the extent they think
about it, they'd say no,

01:14:29.550 --> 01:14:31.440
it's a state granted recognition of

01:14:31.440 --> 01:14:32.940
some deeper natural right.

01:14:32.940 --> 01:14:36.000
And I think that people who
either deeply are committed to

01:14:36.000 --> 01:14:37.260
or fetishize the Second Amendment,

01:14:37.260 --> 01:14:39.819
think of it as something like that.

01:14:39.819 --> 01:14:42.840
January 6th is an
interesting counterexample

01:14:42.840 --> 01:14:45.517
'cause I actually do think it's
an example of deputization.

01:14:45.517 --> 01:14:48.390
One of the interesting things
about the January 6th rioters

01:14:48.390 --> 01:14:52.590
is that they resist the phrase
"January 6th rioters," right?

01:14:52.590 --> 01:14:55.230
Indeed, the late breaking news of

01:14:55.230 --> 01:14:56.790
the subpoena of the former president

01:14:56.790 --> 01:15:00.933
is precisely because, the claim
is, he misled these people

01:15:00.933 --> 01:15:03.492
into thinking they were deputized.

01:15:03.492 --> 01:15:04.325
These were not people who said

01:15:04.325 --> 01:15:06.332
"something's gone wrong with the state,

01:15:06.332 --> 01:15:07.578
thus I'm overthrowing it."

01:15:07.578 --> 01:15:09.032
These were people who

01:15:09.032 --> 01:15:10.770
it is deeply important to them
that they hold onto the claim

01:15:10.770 --> 01:15:13.260
that they're patriots
because they were deputized.

01:15:13.260 --> 01:15:15.390
And my point is that there's
something important about that.

01:15:15.390 --> 01:15:17.190
They wouldn't hold onto it so dearly

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:18.660
if it weren't important.

01:15:18.660 --> 01:15:19.590
And then lastly, I wanna say

01:15:19.590 --> 01:15:21.950
I think that's the end of
the spectrum I wanna get to,

01:15:21.950 --> 01:15:24.360
when people feel authorized themselves

01:15:24.360 --> 01:15:26.191
to use their violence.

01:15:26.191 --> 01:15:27.660
And I wanna say it's even
different than self-defense.

01:15:27.660 --> 01:15:29.040
Again, it's a spectrum.

01:15:29.040 --> 01:15:30.570
One of the things that's difficult about

01:15:30.570 --> 01:15:34.094
many instances of violence on Black people

01:15:34.094 --> 01:15:36.840
is that it's described as self-defense.

01:15:36.840 --> 01:15:38.760
And we know that the self-defense

01:15:38.760 --> 01:15:41.310
is deeply racially refracted, right?

01:15:41.310 --> 01:15:45.270
That the Black man sneezed
and thus self-defense.

01:15:45.270 --> 01:15:46.380
But what I wanna say is

01:15:46.380 --> 01:15:48.960
that's what frightens people

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:50.520
when it comes to racial violence.

01:15:50.520 --> 01:15:52.740
It's not just that you
might be violent towards me

01:15:52.740 --> 01:15:54.360
or you might want my wallet.

01:15:54.360 --> 01:15:56.093
That happens in the world.

01:15:56.093 --> 01:15:58.795
It's that you feel authorized to do so.

01:15:58.795 --> 01:15:59.834
And that's what I wanna pick out

01:15:59.834 --> 01:16:02.160
that I think is on the
further end of the spectrum.

01:16:02.160 --> 01:16:03.820
- Question in terms of
language is about the word,

01:16:03.820 --> 01:16:05.850
first of all entitlement.

01:16:05.850 --> 01:16:09.000
But also I keep thinking gender
and violence against women

01:16:09.000 --> 01:16:11.640
and the gender of the interaction

01:16:11.640 --> 01:16:14.801
as needing to be engaged as the authority

01:16:14.801 --> 01:16:18.332
to be violent against a
person in your household.

01:16:18.332 --> 01:16:19.590
- Yeah.

01:16:19.590 --> 01:16:22.358
- And how that form of entitlement plays

01:16:22.358 --> 01:16:26.520
and whether you wanna,
deputizing maybe a subset of

01:16:26.520 --> 01:16:31.520
an entitlement to engage,
some of which could come from,

01:16:32.017 --> 01:16:36.690
so there's obviously more we
want to probe in trying to

01:16:36.690 --> 01:16:41.430
understand the parameters
of the imagery and claim.

01:16:41.430 --> 01:16:42.480
- That's really helpful.

01:16:42.480 --> 01:16:44.597
I don't wanna dominate this,

01:16:44.597 --> 01:16:46.029
but I would say that's
really helpful because

01:16:46.029 --> 01:16:50.216
I think you might think, for
example, that violent men

01:16:50.216 --> 01:16:53.730
dispensing violence
against women 200 years ago

01:16:53.730 --> 01:16:56.610
was seen as both entitled and deputized.

01:16:56.610 --> 01:16:58.590
And now some men still
think it's entitled,

01:16:58.590 --> 01:17:00.600
but I think very few men
think it's deputized.

01:17:00.600 --> 01:17:02.197
Very few men would say

01:17:02.197 --> 01:17:05.220
"actually the state would
authorize this" any longer.

01:17:05.220 --> 01:17:07.080
- [Judith] Unless we think
of abortion, but let's go-

01:17:07.080 --> 01:17:08.879
- Yeah.
(audience awwing)

01:17:08.879 --> 01:17:11.611
- [Audience Member #1] And very quickly,

01:17:11.611 --> 01:17:16.611
listening to Ekow talk
and then Caleb's response,

01:17:17.250 --> 01:17:18.840
I just couldn't help but contribute

01:17:18.840 --> 01:17:23.400
something minor thinking
about deputization.

01:17:23.400 --> 01:17:24.657
I realized that

01:17:24.657 --> 01:17:29.657
reading "Racecraft" by
Barbara and Karen Fields

01:17:29.700 --> 01:17:33.810
was the first time in my life
when somebody explained to me

01:17:33.810 --> 01:17:37.230
so clearly what ideology is,

01:17:37.230 --> 01:17:39.720
and if I have their definition correct,

01:17:39.720 --> 01:17:44.280
ideology is not something that
you memorize and then repeat.

01:17:44.280 --> 01:17:48.600
It has to be enacted over
and over and over again

01:17:48.600 --> 01:17:50.550
to do its real work.

01:17:50.550 --> 01:17:52.787
So I was thinking deputization is

01:17:52.787 --> 01:17:56.520
the enactment of ideology
over and over again

01:17:56.520 --> 01:17:59.760
regardless of the four
types you just cited,

01:17:59.760 --> 01:18:04.760
what it's doing is spreading
and reproducing itself.

01:18:05.190 --> 01:18:09.510
And like I was, I'm sorry,
I'm live in Los Angeles,

01:18:09.510 --> 01:18:13.020
but the president of our
city council Neri Martinez

01:18:13.020 --> 01:18:16.110
said on tape, which was
reproduced on Reddit,

01:18:16.110 --> 01:18:18.210
that her fellow councilman Mike Bonin,

01:18:18.210 --> 01:18:19.890
I'm sorry, it's very disturbing,

01:18:19.890 --> 01:18:24.000
his adopted Black son, she
said she needed to be able to

01:18:24.000 --> 01:18:28.140
take him around the
corner and beat his ass.

01:18:28.140 --> 01:18:30.390
This is, we're talking
about a two-year-old child.

01:18:30.390 --> 01:18:33.810
This, I'm sorry, this is very,
very disturbing, but it is,

01:18:33.810 --> 01:18:37.578
I think the reproduction of ideology

01:18:37.578 --> 01:18:41.310
in her self-deputization
casually in that moment.

01:18:41.310 --> 01:18:44.250
And so she's even calling
upon an imaginary space

01:18:44.250 --> 01:18:47.310
where people are continuing
to do this kind of work.

01:18:47.310 --> 01:18:48.660
I think maybe in a way,

01:18:48.660 --> 01:18:52.830
does it relate to Elizabeth
Hinton's wonderful piece

01:18:52.830 --> 01:18:54.630
about the protest movement?

01:18:54.630 --> 01:18:57.840
Are we also, like, why do we
have to go out in the streets

01:18:57.840 --> 01:19:00.090
over and over and over again

01:19:00.090 --> 01:19:03.570
every time we witness
state-sanction violence?

01:19:03.570 --> 01:19:08.163
Is it also our work to
reproduce a counter-ideology?

01:19:09.570 --> 01:19:12.275
- Obviously I wanna let
Elizabeth (indistinct),

01:19:12.275 --> 01:19:14.278
but I just want, first of
all, thank you for that.

01:19:14.278 --> 01:19:17.256
I just wanna add one last
smallest of corrections

01:19:17.256 --> 01:19:19.830
is that Jacob, his son,
is actually eight now.

01:19:19.830 --> 01:19:22.449
And I know this 'cause Mike and Sean

01:19:22.449 --> 01:19:24.019
are actually friends of mine.

01:19:24.019 --> 01:19:25.829
- [Audience Member #1]
Oh, they keep saying that

01:19:25.829 --> 01:19:27.440
he was two or three years
old and this was a year ago.

01:19:27.440 --> 01:19:30.215
- So, they're friends of mine actually.

01:19:30.215 --> 01:19:31.860
And so I've had to live, I
mean, I don't pretend it's,

01:19:31.860 --> 01:19:35.280
but you know, it's been painful
to be in touch with them.

01:19:35.280 --> 01:19:36.750
I remember when they adopted Jacob

01:19:36.750 --> 01:19:38.970
and I played with Jacob in their backyard

01:19:38.970 --> 01:19:40.530
and that kid is loved.

01:19:40.530 --> 01:19:41.363
Sorry.

01:19:42.956 --> 01:19:45.757
- Thank you for the question,
and I think in some ways

01:19:45.757 --> 01:19:46.680
it relates to the points

01:19:46.680 --> 01:19:49.170
that Tracey was making about fugitivity.

01:19:49.170 --> 01:19:51.540
Unfortunately I was unable to hear

01:19:51.540 --> 01:19:54.394
Professor James's remarks,

01:19:54.394 --> 01:19:57.060
I couldn't make it to the morning panel.

01:19:57.060 --> 01:20:01.230
But I think, you know, the
example of the kids is this,

01:20:01.230 --> 01:20:05.040
they literally allowed
the kids in Baltimore,

01:20:05.040 --> 01:20:09.300
allowed the suspects
who were being arrested

01:20:09.300 --> 01:20:10.890
to become fugitives, right?

01:20:10.890 --> 01:20:13.497
I mean, I think, you know,

01:20:13.497 --> 01:20:14.700
and that's in some ways
the point that I ended on.

01:20:14.700 --> 01:20:19.700
As long as we have violence
sanctioned by the state,

01:20:20.397 --> 01:20:22.080
the state has a monopoly on violence,

01:20:22.080 --> 01:20:27.080
and with that, the people,
white people in particular,

01:20:27.330 --> 01:20:29.891
are deputized to enact violence.

01:20:29.891 --> 01:20:34.620
And I think, you know, in
response to Caleb's point

01:20:34.620 --> 01:20:37.654
and also thinking about
deputization and January 6th,

01:20:37.654 --> 01:20:41.670
you know, it's about white supremacy,

01:20:41.670 --> 01:20:43.599
white supremacist ideology

01:20:43.599 --> 01:20:48.321
that is slippery in terms
of its ties to the state.

01:20:48.321 --> 01:20:53.287
So, you know, when
Donald Trump tells people

01:20:53.287 --> 01:20:55.140
"Come to the Capitol, it's gonna be wild,"

01:20:55.140 --> 01:20:56.550
storm the capital,

01:20:56.550 --> 01:20:59.520
that's that state-sanctioned
white supremacist violence,

01:20:59.520 --> 01:21:02.836
which has always been part
of the American state.

01:21:02.836 --> 01:21:07.796
But as long as we're
continuing to live in this

01:21:07.796 --> 01:21:11.490
where some groups of people are fugitives,

01:21:11.490 --> 01:21:14.488
some groups of people are captive,

01:21:14.488 --> 01:21:18.220
then it is, you know, the responsibility

01:21:18.220 --> 01:21:23.220
and a natural response of those
conditions to contest them.

01:21:23.250 --> 01:21:24.240
I mean, one of the big things

01:21:24.240 --> 01:21:26.550
I didn't get into it in this talk,

01:21:26.550 --> 01:21:29.430
but the book "America on Fire"

01:21:29.430 --> 01:21:31.470
from which I draw in this work

01:21:31.470 --> 01:21:35.070
is that violence precipitates violence,

01:21:35.070 --> 01:21:38.220
it's a cycle of violence,
of state-sanctioned violence

01:21:38.220 --> 01:21:39.855
and then community violence,

01:21:39.855 --> 01:21:42.360
insurgency and counter-insurgency

01:21:42.360 --> 01:21:45.646
that we've been stuck in

01:21:45.646 --> 01:21:50.646
because of our economic systems,
our ideologies, et cetera.

01:21:52.327 --> 01:21:54.930
- [Judith] We started
a couple minutes late,

01:21:54.930 --> 01:21:59.522
so I'm gonna invade lunch
a teeny bit and invite,

01:21:59.522 --> 01:22:02.550
and I think the way to do it
is there are a few hands up.

01:22:02.550 --> 01:22:05.640
So we're gonna take the hands
that are up and listen to you

01:22:05.640 --> 01:22:08.100
and then everybody will
get their closing comments

01:22:08.100 --> 01:22:10.470
in relationship to you and otherwise.

01:22:10.470 --> 01:22:11.970
And the people with microphones

01:22:11.970 --> 01:22:14.221
are better than me at running around.

01:22:14.221 --> 01:22:15.756
Please introduce yourselves,

01:22:15.756 --> 01:22:17.875
but please put your hand up high

01:22:17.875 --> 01:22:20.820
and so we can grab all your comments

01:22:20.820 --> 01:22:23.220
and then have a response.

01:22:23.220 --> 01:22:24.360
Go ahead, start.

01:22:24.360 --> 01:22:26.430
- [Rich] Hi, my name is Rich Viada,

01:22:26.430 --> 01:22:29.607
I'm with the Prison
Education Program at NYU

01:22:29.607 --> 01:22:34.607
and I wanna say thank you
for this entire day, really.

01:22:35.008 --> 01:22:37.380
I am one of the coordinators there.

01:22:37.380 --> 01:22:41.520
I do reentry work and I'm also a poet,

01:22:41.520 --> 01:22:42.810
and indeed I'm a poet that is

01:22:42.810 --> 01:22:44.792
steeped in a lot of those traditions

01:22:44.792 --> 01:22:46.410
that came out of those rebellions,

01:22:46.410 --> 01:22:48.760
that were spoken of so eloquently,

01:22:48.760 --> 01:22:51.210
during the sixties and seventies.

01:22:51.210 --> 01:22:55.650
And as such, our literature,
Nuyorican literature,

01:22:55.650 --> 01:22:57.150
Black arts movement, Black nationalism,

01:22:57.150 --> 01:22:59.700
a lot of that stuff is informed by

01:22:59.700 --> 01:23:02.019
writers who were formerly incarcerated.

01:23:02.019 --> 01:23:06.060
And one thing I haven't heard
yet, unless I missed it,

01:23:06.060 --> 01:23:10.500
is the literary imagination
from that liminal space, right?

01:23:10.500 --> 01:23:13.362
From the space that is not incarcerated,

01:23:13.362 --> 01:23:17.310
but who is bringing the
experience of trauma,

01:23:17.310 --> 01:23:18.390
and I do social work, right?

01:23:18.390 --> 01:23:20.010
So trauma's always part of that.

01:23:20.010 --> 01:23:23.062
The experience of trauma and the time lost

01:23:23.062 --> 01:23:27.614
into a world that has
in large part moved on

01:23:27.614 --> 01:23:30.930
or moved away or moved
in different directions.

01:23:30.930 --> 01:23:33.540
So I'm curious, in any of your readings,

01:23:33.540 --> 01:23:36.270
if you've come across,
and we're gonna hear from

01:23:36.270 --> 01:23:39.540
formerly-incarcerated writers
later, which is awesome,

01:23:39.540 --> 01:23:42.957
but my question to you is
about the theory behind it

01:23:42.957 --> 01:23:44.190
and some of the thinking that

01:23:44.190 --> 01:23:46.110
maybe some folks have put on paper

01:23:46.110 --> 01:23:48.930
around writing from that space.

01:23:48.930 --> 01:23:50.670
And I'm curious if any of your readings

01:23:50.670 --> 01:23:55.050
you've come across, and this
could mean centuries ago.

01:23:55.050 --> 01:24:00.050
I don't know of writers who
are talking about that process

01:24:00.121 --> 01:24:02.370
and I wonder if there might be

01:24:02.370 --> 01:24:05.010
a recommendation for a reading or two

01:24:05.010 --> 01:24:08.253
for someone who has students
who are writing from there.

01:24:12.178 --> 01:24:16.740
(panelists speaking faintly)

01:24:16.740 --> 01:24:18.690
- [Sonali] Hi, my name
is Sonali Chakravarti,

01:24:18.690 --> 01:24:20.520
I'm from Wesleyan University,

01:24:20.520 --> 01:24:22.380
and my research is about juries,

01:24:22.380 --> 01:24:24.450
which are a very specific type of public

01:24:24.450 --> 01:24:27.201
whose imagination of
prisons and of defendants

01:24:27.201 --> 01:24:29.198
carries a lot of weight.

01:24:29.198 --> 01:24:32.100
And I'm curious if the panelists feel that

01:24:32.100 --> 01:24:33.360
there might be any movement in

01:24:33.360 --> 01:24:35.737
the way jurors in this country understand

01:24:35.737 --> 01:24:38.678
the different aspects of this story.

01:24:38.678 --> 01:24:41.970
And in addition to that,
there's a huge need to have

01:24:41.970 --> 01:24:44.373
more racially-diverse juries certainly,

01:24:45.285 --> 01:24:46.230
but I'm just also curious if you see

01:24:46.230 --> 01:24:51.123
the possibilities of change
in the imagination of jurors.

01:24:52.180 --> 01:24:55.059
- I think there were a
couple other hands over here.

01:24:55.059 --> 01:24:57.476
(indistinct)

01:24:58.497 --> 01:25:03.360
(panelists speaking indistinctly)

01:25:03.360 --> 01:25:05.430
- So mine was kind of related to,

01:25:05.430 --> 01:25:07.198
and thank you Professor Hinton.

01:25:07.198 --> 01:25:09.560
(Nasser chuckling)

01:25:09.560 --> 01:25:12.720
But I'm Nasser, Yale undergrad.

01:25:12.720 --> 01:25:15.660
So I guess my question, it
was kind of thinking with

01:25:15.660 --> 01:25:17.970
the January 6th riots.

01:25:17.970 --> 01:25:22.970
So I remember, I was
actually my APUSH class

01:25:23.220 --> 01:25:25.762
and we were like watching it live

01:25:25.762 --> 01:25:28.740
and we saw the gallows for Mike Pence.

01:25:28.740 --> 01:25:30.258
And I was thinking too,

01:25:30.258 --> 01:25:33.270
the same people who
were motivated that day

01:25:33.270 --> 01:25:35.250
had the gallows for Obama

01:25:35.250 --> 01:25:38.490
and actually putting like
dolls of him being hung.

01:25:38.490 --> 01:25:43.207
And I guess you refer to
him as a secular king.

01:25:45.180 --> 01:25:46.470
I thought that was interesting.

01:25:46.470 --> 01:25:51.470
And I guess, I'm just trying
to think out loud here.

01:25:52.110 --> 01:25:55.740
So it was Obama, he was like in Indonesia,

01:25:55.740 --> 01:25:58.800
he was even going to, the
mother says, Islamic schools,

01:25:58.800 --> 01:26:01.830
he's Kenyan, he has a Muslim middle name,

01:26:01.830 --> 01:26:04.350
and he's the head of a white government.

01:26:04.350 --> 01:26:07.320
And that must have caused
confusion for people who were,

01:26:07.320 --> 01:26:09.930
you know, as you were saying,

01:26:09.930 --> 01:26:12.090
being sanctified to use this violence.

01:26:12.090 --> 01:26:16.381
And I guess maybe they
felt betrayed by Mike Pence

01:26:16.381 --> 01:26:17.957
(Nasser chuckling lightly)

01:26:17.957 --> 01:26:19.110
and that he deserved,
that Mike Pence deserved

01:26:19.110 --> 01:26:21.570
the same punishment as
someone who was Black.

01:26:21.570 --> 01:26:24.330
But Obama might not be,
like, African-American.

01:26:24.330 --> 01:26:28.093
He's like a, he's like an
Eastern Black, you know?

01:26:28.093 --> 01:26:30.720
And I want to see how
that confusion led to

01:26:30.720 --> 01:26:34.350
the same resulting anger and punishment

01:26:34.350 --> 01:26:38.070
through such a very American
violent way of punishment,

01:26:38.070 --> 01:26:39.170
which is the lynching.

01:26:40.158 --> 01:26:41.423
So that was what I was wondering.

01:26:42.839 --> 01:26:43.897
- Thank you.

01:26:43.897 --> 01:26:46.701
There are hands from the
back, at least two or one.

01:26:46.701 --> 01:26:49.552
(panelists speaking faintly)

01:26:49.552 --> 01:26:51.070
- [Audience Member #2] So,
thank you for everything.

01:26:52.001 --> 01:26:53.617
- Scream a little bit,
it's a little (indistinct).

01:26:53.617 --> 01:26:55.298
- [Audience Member #2]
Okay, you can hear me now?

01:26:55.298 --> 01:26:56.248
So thanks to the panel.

01:26:56.248 --> 01:26:57.495
- The room doesn't have perfect acoustics.

01:26:57.495 --> 01:26:58.328
- [Audience Member #2] Okay.

01:26:58.328 --> 01:27:00.660
So I was wondering if
you could say more about

01:27:00.660 --> 01:27:03.780
the distinction between
self-defense and violence.

01:27:03.780 --> 01:27:07.710
I know seminarians who created
nonprofits in New York City

01:27:07.710 --> 01:27:10.590
around domestic violence,
intimate violence.

01:27:10.590 --> 01:27:13.050
And the statement, along
with some Buddhists, is

01:27:13.050 --> 01:27:16.110
you can use force against violence,

01:27:16.110 --> 01:27:19.440
but force is not a synonym for violence.

01:27:19.440 --> 01:27:22.323
So then self-defense
has a distinct category.

01:27:24.263 --> 01:27:25.907
- That's interesting.

01:27:25.907 --> 01:27:27.498
- I think there's one more
hand there. Yes, please.

01:27:27.498 --> 01:27:29.490
- [Andrew] Hi, Andrew Clark from Yukon.

01:27:29.490 --> 01:27:33.808
I'm interested in the
concept of punishment,

01:27:33.808 --> 01:27:37.080
and Judith, in your initial slides

01:27:37.080 --> 01:27:38.220
when you talked about prison,

01:27:38.220 --> 01:27:40.800
there's a lot of
punishment that's in there.

01:27:40.800 --> 01:27:44.040
And then the idea, Elizabeth,
that you talked about,

01:27:44.040 --> 01:27:46.560
the crucible period and
the hyper-incarceration

01:27:46.560 --> 01:27:50.733
which infused punishment even
more into the carceral space.

01:27:51.799 --> 01:27:55.440
And then the idea of deputizing

01:27:55.440 --> 01:27:59.250
and who gets deputized
to punish within prison.

01:27:59.250 --> 01:28:02.220
And then going to the fiction narrative of

01:28:02.220 --> 01:28:04.020
how can we change this?

01:28:04.020 --> 01:28:06.120
And when we're looking at other countries

01:28:06.120 --> 01:28:10.170
that don't view prison
as a punishment scheme,

01:28:10.170 --> 01:28:12.990
but see as the taking away
of liberty as punishment

01:28:12.990 --> 01:28:16.110
and the role of a prison
guard or correction system

01:28:16.110 --> 01:28:17.910
is actually rehabilitation,

01:28:17.910 --> 01:28:20.130
is there a way to change that narrative

01:28:20.130 --> 01:28:24.270
from someone that's been
deputized to punish,

01:28:24.270 --> 01:28:27.750
to be deputized to actually help?

01:28:27.750 --> 01:28:29.350
- Well, Tracey, do you wanna go?

01:28:30.612 --> 01:28:32.133
Let's be geographic and go back,

01:28:32.133 --> 01:28:33.657
everybody who has any comments

01:28:33.657 --> 01:28:36.870
whether intersecting with these questions

01:28:36.870 --> 01:28:41.060
or the exchange among the
panels, if we go back this way.

01:28:41.060 --> 01:28:44.250
- I don't have any answers
to those questions.

01:28:44.250 --> 01:28:46.777
- You can respond to any of the comments.

01:28:46.777 --> 01:28:49.082
(panelists chattering)

01:28:49.082 --> 01:28:51.690
- I don't know, I have
to think about it more.

01:28:51.690 --> 01:28:52.967
- Okay.

01:28:52.967 --> 01:28:55.339
(panelists chuckling)

01:28:55.339 --> 01:28:56.422
- So Zachary?

01:28:57.827 --> 01:29:01.053
I know there's a lot out there, so.

01:29:01.895 --> 01:29:02.728
- There's a lot out there.

01:29:04.357 --> 01:29:06.078
- So you get to select something.

01:29:06.078 --> 01:29:07.222
(indistinct)

01:29:07.222 --> 01:29:09.138
- (indistinct)

01:29:09.138 --> 01:29:11.442
I think we've been talking a
lot about language actually.

01:29:11.442 --> 01:29:13.830
I think we've been talking about, oh,

01:29:13.830 --> 01:29:15.930
I can't remember what
Joey's point was just now

01:29:15.930 --> 01:29:18.033
about distinguishing violence and force,

01:29:18.960 --> 01:29:20.490
using the word deputization

01:29:20.490 --> 01:29:24.360
to pinpoint a number of
things with one word.

01:29:24.360 --> 01:29:26.970
And I think what I'd like
to say about that is just,

01:29:26.970 --> 01:29:28.902
is pretty simple,

01:29:28.902 --> 01:29:31.860
that I think that we live
in a culture in which

01:29:31.860 --> 01:29:35.610
we're bombarded with
images, images, images.

01:29:35.610 --> 01:29:40.610
And I think language is
one way to resist that

01:29:42.390 --> 01:29:44.678
and to get past that.

01:29:44.678 --> 01:29:49.678
And I think you said something interesting

01:29:50.550 --> 01:29:52.759
about senses in general,

01:29:52.759 --> 01:29:57.759
and I was just reading this
book about Jewish mysticism

01:29:59.550 --> 01:30:03.790
and it's talking about how

01:30:04.690 --> 01:30:07.980
there's a difference between
wisdom and understanding.

01:30:07.980 --> 01:30:12.507
And that wisdom is a flash of knowledge.

01:30:15.317 --> 01:30:20.317
It's God is sending some
sort of energy that gives you

01:30:20.340 --> 01:30:21.780
this flash of knowledge,

01:30:21.780 --> 01:30:23.430
which is sort of I guess like intuition,

01:30:23.430 --> 01:30:27.343
and then past that is understanding,

01:30:27.343 --> 01:30:29.820
which is when you take that flash

01:30:29.820 --> 01:30:33.914
and you analyze it and particularize it.

01:30:33.914 --> 01:30:36.510
And that's considered a next phase,

01:30:36.510 --> 01:30:40.050
that's a higher phase than
wisdom, actually understanding.

01:30:40.050 --> 01:30:43.320
And I think that's what
language is helping us to do.

01:30:43.320 --> 01:30:46.290
And I think that that's
what this symposium

01:30:46.290 --> 01:30:49.323
and things like it are
are useful in doing.

01:30:54.990 --> 01:30:56.220
- I've been given a lot of time,

01:30:56.220 --> 01:30:58.440
so I won't take too much more.

01:30:58.440 --> 01:31:01.590
On your, first of all, thank
you for your wonderful work.

01:31:01.590 --> 01:31:04.890
I don't know a lot about
people who've written inside,

01:31:04.890 --> 01:31:07.500
but Dr. James has a set of anthologies

01:31:07.500 --> 01:31:09.210
which I just became aware of.

01:31:09.210 --> 01:31:10.920
So you might wanna speak to her

01:31:10.920 --> 01:31:13.721
or just Google her and buy
everything she's written.

01:31:13.721 --> 01:31:15.277
(panelists chuckling)

01:31:15.277 --> 01:31:17.413
Also an idea, I'm planning on doing so.

01:31:17.413 --> 01:31:19.710
I should say Professor and Doctor James.

01:31:19.710 --> 01:31:23.083
But there's been so much
offered, I just wanna say

01:31:23.083 --> 01:31:25.320
Elizabeth, I love this question of

01:31:25.320 --> 01:31:26.730
the kids fighting in Baltimore,

01:31:26.730 --> 01:31:28.860
allowing others to become fugitive.

01:31:28.860 --> 01:31:31.821
Sonali, you know how much I
appreciate your work on juries.

01:31:31.821 --> 01:31:32.760
I really want to think about that.

01:31:32.760 --> 01:31:35.380
I actually think that's
a really important one,

01:31:35.380 --> 01:31:37.230
and the way in which, so
when I teach criminal law,

01:31:37.230 --> 01:31:39.617
there are just a lot of stories about

01:31:39.617 --> 01:31:42.023
the disconnect between jurors

01:31:42.023 --> 01:31:45.917
in particular when the juries
are not racially diverse.

01:31:45.917 --> 01:31:48.480
And now you're starting
to see that change.

01:31:48.480 --> 01:31:50.212
You pointed out,

01:31:50.212 --> 01:31:54.075
something odd about the Rodney King video,

01:31:54.075 --> 01:31:56.507
the analogy I always make is

01:31:56.507 --> 01:31:58.800
if you finally believed your wife

01:31:58.800 --> 01:32:01.170
after she complained
for years about her boss

01:32:01.170 --> 01:32:02.520
because you saw the video,

01:32:02.520 --> 01:32:04.020
your relationship would've both taken

01:32:04.020 --> 01:32:06.680
a step forward and a step back, right?

01:32:06.680 --> 01:32:08.197
'Cause it's sort of like,

01:32:08.197 --> 01:32:09.780
"Well, I complained to
you since time immemorial,

01:32:09.780 --> 01:32:11.882
but you had to see the video."

01:32:11.882 --> 01:32:13.282
So I think it's complicated.

01:32:14.130 --> 01:32:16.680
On the Obama point, I
think it's a great point,

01:32:16.680 --> 01:32:18.212
thank you for that.

01:32:18.212 --> 01:32:19.080
What I wanna say is something like

01:32:20.128 --> 01:32:21.416
I think you can be patriotic

01:32:21.416 --> 01:32:22.471
without thinking you're authorized.

01:32:22.471 --> 01:32:24.240
So you can be a patriot of
a lost state, for example.

01:32:24.240 --> 01:32:25.590
There are people who thought
they were fighting for

01:32:25.590 --> 01:32:28.019
the state that was taken away from them.

01:32:28.019 --> 01:32:28.852
And there are people who
think they're authorized

01:32:28.852 --> 01:32:30.990
by the current state, and
I wanna disentangle that.

01:32:30.990 --> 01:32:32.700
And likewise, I wanna disentangle

01:32:32.700 --> 01:32:35.682
self-defense versus violence
along the same lines.

01:32:35.682 --> 01:32:40.082
I think self-defense comes
closer to the idea of force,

01:32:40.082 --> 01:32:42.660
the idea that it's an
innate authorization,

01:32:42.660 --> 01:32:44.580
whereas violence is more
what I'm pointing at

01:32:44.580 --> 01:32:45.413
in deputization.

01:32:46.436 --> 01:32:47.637
I'll stop there.

01:32:47.637 --> 01:32:48.470
Thank you all for your comments,
they've been tremendous.

01:32:49.675 --> 01:32:51.183
- Yeah, yeah.

01:32:51.183 --> 01:32:53.190
I just, this has been a
really enriching conversation.

01:32:53.190 --> 01:32:55.500
So I wanna thank my fellow
panelists once again

01:32:55.500 --> 01:32:57.780
and all of you for your really
thought-provoking questions.

01:32:57.780 --> 01:33:00.150
I guess just picking up on the last one

01:33:00.150 --> 01:33:04.230
in terms of trying to end
maybe on a hopeful note,

01:33:04.230 --> 01:33:06.360
thinking about what help might look like,

01:33:06.360 --> 01:33:08.550
how we can reframe some of these things,

01:33:08.550 --> 01:33:11.280
it picks up on my last comment

01:33:11.280 --> 01:33:16.280
in the sense, you know, we
know what the solutions are.

01:33:16.770 --> 01:33:18.333
I mean that's, you know,

01:33:18.333 --> 01:33:21.870
the people who felt like
they had no other recourse

01:33:21.870 --> 01:33:26.870
but to enact various forms
of violence against the state

01:33:27.510 --> 01:33:30.180
knew that the solution was

01:33:30.180 --> 01:33:32.880
resources into under-resourced communities

01:33:32.880 --> 01:33:36.313
and jobs and better
schools and redistribution.

01:33:36.313 --> 01:33:40.350
the Kerner Commission in
'68 says all of this, right?

01:33:40.350 --> 01:33:41.670
And so, I mean, I think that's

01:33:41.670 --> 01:33:44.010
why the questions of this
conference are so interesting

01:33:44.010 --> 01:33:47.860
because in order to actually bring about

01:33:47.860 --> 01:33:50.580
the kind of form of governance
that would be necessary

01:33:50.580 --> 01:33:52.260
to do that, which is, you know,

01:33:52.260 --> 01:33:54.480
what I think Defund is really all about,

01:33:54.480 --> 01:33:58.080
calling for a different
approach to governance

01:33:58.080 --> 01:34:01.200
given that these punitive strategies

01:34:01.200 --> 01:34:05.548
have failed to actually
keep communities safe.

01:34:05.548 --> 01:34:10.548
How are we going to then
move the public imagination

01:34:11.137 --> 01:34:15.930
to be able to not 'other' people,

01:34:15.930 --> 01:34:20.930
to de-link people of color, Black people

01:34:21.420 --> 01:34:23.988
from ideas about criminality

01:34:23.988 --> 01:34:27.300
and to make more visible perhaps

01:34:27.300 --> 01:34:29.578
in order to raise that awareness,

01:34:29.578 --> 01:34:33.690
these underlying problems
and the failed policies.

01:34:33.690 --> 01:34:37.680
So, you know, in a lot of ways
that's the task before us.

01:34:37.680 --> 01:34:41.807
But the imagination is so key within that.

01:34:41.807 --> 01:34:43.620
- So, you raised, I mean,

01:34:43.620 --> 01:34:47.384
I think this worlds of
language in act, and thinking

01:34:47.384 --> 01:34:52.101
what these meanings and
sensory experiences are.

01:34:52.101 --> 01:34:54.870
And there's this idea of the sovereign,

01:34:54.870 --> 01:34:57.300
whether the deputy is the quasi-sovereign

01:34:57.300 --> 01:35:00.390
or the self-understood
sovereign in the household

01:35:00.390 --> 01:35:01.620
and the non-sovereign.

01:35:01.620 --> 01:35:04.733
And the underlying question is
what can we do to each other

01:35:04.733 --> 01:35:07.697
in what level of authority?

01:35:07.697 --> 01:35:11.340
And there are some things
that have been debated

01:35:11.340 --> 01:35:13.238
like whipping or starving

01:35:13.238 --> 01:35:17.322
that for some social orders
we take off the table.

01:35:17.322 --> 01:35:19.350
It isn't that it isn't happening.

01:35:19.350 --> 01:35:22.620
It's that we don't say it's authorized,

01:35:22.620 --> 01:35:25.680
when we disavow that authority.

01:35:25.680 --> 01:35:28.680
And the current struggle
is what do we disavow

01:35:28.680 --> 01:35:32.130
and what do we refund as
well as what do we defund?

01:35:32.130 --> 01:35:35.201
And it's been incredibly moving

01:35:35.201 --> 01:35:38.502
participating in a
conversation with you all

01:35:38.502 --> 01:35:43.502
around these incredibly
weighty topics in a scary time.

01:35:43.715 --> 01:35:48.715
I think I'm supposed to
announce, or actually better yet,

01:35:48.942 --> 01:35:51.203
well, we wanna first of all thank Diane.

01:35:51.203 --> 01:35:53.278
And also I wanna thank,

01:35:53.278 --> 01:35:56.047
you've been incredibly
logistically engaged

01:35:56.047 --> 01:36:00.416
and in terms of when you were
saying things just happen,

01:36:00.416 --> 01:36:02.220
things don't just happen.

01:36:02.220 --> 01:36:04.890
They happen because people work
hard at making them happen.

01:36:04.890 --> 01:36:07.178
So I think we all need
to pause to thank you.

01:36:07.178 --> 01:36:12.178
(audience applauding)
(panelists applauding)

01:36:12.743 --> 01:36:15.993
(gentle ambient music)

